-- NEET UG previous-year questions (official NTA papers), 2017.
-- Question text is the exam's; answers are solved and cross-checked independently by UG Prep
-- (not the official key); one-liners and explanations are original. Idempotent by id.
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, exam, subject, topic, stem, options, correct_option_id, one_liner,
  explanation, distractors, high_yield, difficulty, is_pyq, pyq_year, repeat_count, source, image_url)
VALUES
('c4c810f2-8500-5963-b3f7-138f23851983', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biomolecules', 'Which one of the following statements is correct, with reference to enzymes?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Apoenzyme = Holoenzyme + Coenzyme"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Holoenzyme = Apoenzyme + Coenzyme"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Coenzyme = Apoenzyme + Holoenzyme"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Holoenzyme = Coenzyme + Co-factor"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The complete catalytically active enzyme (holoenzyme) is the protein part (apoenzyme) plus its non-protein cofactor such as a coenzyme.', 'An apoenzyme is the inactive protein portion of a conjugated enzyme. It becomes active only when bound to its cofactor (coenzyme, prosthetic group or metal ion). The active combination is called the holoenzyme.', '{"a": "Reverses the relationship; the apoenzyme is a part of the holoenzyme, not the sum.", "c": "A coenzyme is the small organic cofactor, not the sum of apoenzyme and holoenzyme.", "d": "A coenzyme is itself a type of cofactor; the protein part (apoenzyme) is missing."}'::jsonb, 'Catalytic activity is lost when the cofactor is removed from the enzyme.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('9605a526-4650-5c46-ace5-c168266b16fb', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants', 'Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) is the primary CO₂ acceptor in:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "C₃ plants"}, {"id": "b", "text": "C₄ plants"}, {"id": "c", "text": "C₂ plants"}, {"id": "d", "text": "C₃ and C₄ plants"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'In C₄ plants PEP in mesophyll cells is the primary CO₂ acceptor, forming the 4-carbon oxaloacetic acid.', 'C₄ plants fix CO₂ first in mesophyll cells using PEP carboxylase, which carboxylates PEP to OAA. C₃ plants use RuBP as the primary acceptor via RuBisCO. RuBP acts as the secondary acceptor in the bundle sheath cells of C₄ plants.', '{"a": "C₃ plants use RuBP as the primary CO₂ acceptor.", "c": "''C₂'' refers to photorespiration, which is not CO₂ fixation by PEP.", "d": "Only C₄ plants use PEP as the primary acceptor."}'::jsonb, 'PEP carboxylase (PEPcase) is absent in the mesophyll of C₃ plants and lacks oxygenase activity.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('1f738ec0-c6b0-59f2-a2b6-ac15e00ab15c', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Reproductive Health', 'Match the following sexually transmitted diseases (Column - I) with their causative agent (Column - II) and select the correct option.
Column - I | Column - II
(a) Gonorrhea | (i) HIV
(b) Syphilis | (ii) Neisseria
(c) Genital Warts | (iii) Treponema
(d) AIDS | (iv) Human Papilloma - Virus', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Gonorrhoea is caused by Neisseria, syphilis by Treponema, genital warts by HPV and AIDS by HIV.', 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae causes gonorrhoea and Treponema pallidum causes syphilis; both are bacteria. Genital warts are caused by the human papilloma virus, and AIDS by the human immunodeficiency virus. This gives ii, iii, iv, i.', '{"b": "Assigns Treponema to gonorrhoea and HPV to syphilis, both wrong.", "c": "Assigns HPV to gonorrhoea and Neisseria to syphilis.", "d": "Gonorrhoea is not caused by HPV, and genital warts are not caused by Neisseria."}'::jsonb, 'Hepatitis B, genital herpes and genital warts are STIs that are not completely curable.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('37755973-85a7-50e5-9f06-53b4dec8f19c', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biological Classification', 'Which among the following are the smallest living cells, known without a definite cell wall, pathogenic to plants as well as animals and can survive without oxygen?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Bacillus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pseudomonas"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Mycoplasma"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nostoc"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Mycoplasma are the smallest known living cells; they lack a cell wall and can live anaerobically.', 'Mycoplasma have no cell wall, so they are pleomorphic. They are the smallest living cells known, can survive without oxygen, and cause diseases in plants and animals. The other options all have cell walls.', '{"a": "Bacillus is a walled, rod-shaped bacterium.", "b": "Pseudomonas is a walled, Gram-negative bacterium.", "d": "Nostoc is a walled, filamentous cyanobacterium that photosynthesises."}'::jsonb, 'Mycoplasma are resistant to penicillin because they lack a cell wall.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ea9509d3-0c6d-53f2-8283-a159527ab66a', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 'Which one from those given below is the period for Mendel''s hybridization experiments?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1856 - 1863"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1840 - 1850"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1857 - 1869"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1870 - 1877"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Mendel carried out his pea hybridisation experiments for seven years, from 1856 to 1863.', 'NCERT states that Mendel conducted hybridisation experiments on garden peas for seven years (1856–1863). He proposed the laws of inheritance and published them in 1865/1866.', '{"b": "This is before Mendel began his pea experiments.", "c": "The end year is wrong; the experiments ended in 1863.", "d": "This is after the experiments and the publication."}'::jsonb, 'Mendel''s work was rediscovered in 1900 by de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('0084185f-8542-5cab-a7b2-f54d89b8c264', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 'Flowers which have single ovule in the ovary and are packed into inflorescence are usually pollinated by:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bee"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wind"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bat"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Wind-pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary and many flowers packed into an inflorescence.', 'Wind pollination is non-directional, so wind-pollinated plants produce numerous small flowers in inflorescences (e.g., corn cob, tassels). Each flower usually has a single ovule. They also have light, non-sticky pollen and feathery stigmas.', '{"a": "Water pollination is rare and occurs in a few aquatic plants such as Vallisneria and Zostera; it is not linked to this trait.", "b": "Bee-pollinated flowers are usually large, colourful and nectar-rich, with no such ovule pattern.", "d": "Bat-pollinated flowers are large and showy."}'::jsonb, 'Corn tassels are the wind-blown stigmas and styles that trap pollen.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('e33fa07e-f2d1-5def-8838-e1439cd27025', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Organisms and Populations', 'Asymptote in a logistic growth curve is obtained when:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "The value of ''r'' approaches zero"}, {"id": "b", "text": "K = N"}, {"id": "c", "text": "K > N"}, {"id": "d", "text": "K < N"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The logistic curve levels off (asymptote) when population size N reaches the carrying capacity K.', 'In dN/dt = rN(K−N)/K, the growth rate becomes zero when N = K. The sigmoid curve then flattens into its asymptote at the carrying capacity. r is the intrinsic rate and does not approach zero.', '{"a": "r is the intrinsic rate of natural increase and stays constant; it is the factor (K−N)/K that becomes zero.", "c": "When K > N the population is still growing.", "d": "When N exceeds K the population declines; this is not the asymptote."}'::jsonb, 'Verhulst–Pearl logistic growth is considered more realistic than exponential growth.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('12390aed-9c31-57bd-8117-3a2bd5649103', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Locomotion and Movement', 'Out of ''X'' pairs of ribs in humans only ''Y'' pairs are true ribs. Select the option that correctly represents values of X and Y and provides their explanation:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "X = 12, Y = 7: True ribs are attached dorsally to vertebral column and ventrally to the sternum."}, {"id": "b", "text": "X = 12, Y = 5: True ribs are attached dorsally to vertebral column and sternum on the two ends."}, {"id": "c", "text": "X = 24, Y = 7: True ribs are dorsally attached to vertebral column but are free on ventral side."}, {"id": "d", "text": "X = 24, Y = 12: True ribs are dorsally attached to vertebral column but are free on ventral side."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Humans have 12 pairs of ribs, of which the first 7 pairs are true ribs joined dorsally to the vertebral column and ventrally to the sternum.', 'There are 12 pairs of ribs. Pairs 1–7 are true ribs that attach directly to the sternum through hyaline cartilage. Pairs 8–10 are false (vertebrochondral) ribs and pairs 11–12 are floating ribs that are free ventrally.', '{"b": "There are 7 pairs of true ribs, not 5.", "c": "There are 12 pairs, not 24, and the description given is of floating ribs.", "d": "The numbers are wrong, and true ribs are not free ventrally."}'::jsonb, 'The thoracic vertebrae, ribs and sternum together form the rib cage.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('8f1e2875-7486-57b9-90bc-b7b219119302', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Human Health and Disease', 'MALT constitutes about _______ percent of the lymphoid tissue in human body.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "50%"}, {"id": "b", "text": "20%"}, {"id": "c", "text": "70%"}, {"id": "d", "text": "10%"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue makes up about 50% of the lymphoid tissue in the human body.', 'NCERT notes that lymphoid tissue lies within the lining of the major tracts (respiratory, digestive and urogenital). This mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) constitutes about 50 percent of the body''s lymphoid tissue.', '{"b": "Underestimates the NCERT figure.", "c": "Overestimates the NCERT figure.", "d": "Underestimates the NCERT figure."}'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs; spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, Peyer''s patches and appendix are secondary.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('c6e1c180-b5f0-533e-bf36-b4c15c47d574', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 'Among the following characters, which one was not considered by Mendel in his experiments on pea?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Stem - Tall or Dwarf"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Trichomes - Glandular or non-glandular"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Seed - Green or Yellow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pod - Inflated or Constricted"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Trichome type was not one of Mendel''s seven pea characters.', 'Mendel''s seven traits were stem height, flower colour, flower position, pod shape, pod colour, seed shape and seed colour. Trichomes were not among them.', '{"a": "Stem height (tall/dwarf) was one of Mendel''s characters.", "c": "Seed colour (yellow/green) was one of Mendel''s characters.", "d": "Pod shape (inflated/constricted) was one of Mendel''s characters."}'::jsonb, 'Dominant forms: tall, violet, axial, inflated, green pod, round seed, yellow seed.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ba2e59ce-d390-5d41-a2ea-cf9e5bef5d59', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Cell: The Unit of Life', 'Which of the following cell organelles is responsible for extracting energy from carbohydrates to form ATP?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Lysosome"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ribosome"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Chloroplast"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mitochondrion"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Mitochondria oxidise respiratory substrates such as carbohydrates and trap the energy as ATP.', 'Mitochondria are the site of aerobic respiration, where the Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation produce most of the cell''s ATP. This is why they are called the powerhouses of the cell. Chloroplasts make ATP from light energy, not from carbohydrates.', '{"a": "Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes for intracellular digestion.", "b": "Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis.", "c": "Chloroplasts make ATP by photophosphorylation using light energy; they make carbohydrates rather than break them down."}'::jsonb, 'Mitochondria are semi-autonomous: they have circular DNA and 70S ribosomes.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('96ddec05-2c8c-5a62-b90a-28462aff6b38', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'If there are 999 bases in an RNA that codes for a protein with 333 amino acids, and the base at position 901 is deleted such that the length of the RNA becomes 998 bases, how many codons will be altered?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "11"}, {"id": "c", "text": "33"}, {"id": "d", "text": "333"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'A deletion at base 901 shifts the reading frame from codon 301 onward, so codons 301–333 (33 codons) are altered.', 'Base 901 is the first base of codon 301, since 900/3 = 300 codons lie before it. Deleting it causes a frameshift that alters every codon from 301 to 333. That is 333 − 300 = 33 codons. The first 300 codons are unaffected.', '{"a": "A single altered codon would result from a substitution, not a frameshift deletion.", "b": "The arithmetic is wrong; 33 codons lie downstream of position 900.", "d": "Codons before the deletion site remain unchanged."}'::jsonb, 'Insertions or deletions of one or two bases cause frameshift mutations; three bases add or remove one amino acid.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d8b49499-f5f1-5ad1-a33d-b25bd570154c', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biological Classification', 'Which of the following are found in extreme saline conditions?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Archaebacteria"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Eubacteria"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cyanobacteria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mycobacteria"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Halophilic archaebacteria live in extremely salty habitats.', 'Archaebacteria live in harsh habitats: halophiles in extreme salt, thermoacidophiles in hot springs and methanogens in marshy areas. They can do this because of their distinctive cell wall structure.', '{"b": "Eubacteria are ''true bacteria'' found in ordinary habitats.", "c": "Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic and mostly live in fresh water or on land.", "d": "Mycobacteria are typically pathogens such as M. tuberculosis."}'::jsonb, 'Methanogens in the rumen of cattle produce methane (biogas).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('c33dae8f-73ee-5729-bc3a-d887ed770d0f', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Neural Control and Coordination', 'Receptor sites for neurotransmitters are present on:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "membranes of synaptic vesicles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "pre-synaptic membrane"}, {"id": "c", "text": "tips of axons"}, {"id": "d", "text": "post-synaptic membrane"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Neurotransmitter receptors are located on the post-synaptic membrane.', 'At a chemical synapse the neurotransmitter released from the pre-synaptic terminal diffuses across the cleft. It binds to specific receptors on the post-synaptic membrane, opening ion channels and generating a new potential.', '{"a": "Synaptic vesicles store neurotransmitter; they do not receive it.", "b": "The pre-synaptic membrane releases neurotransmitters by exocytosis.", "c": "Axon tips (terminals) are the releasing side."}'::jsonb, 'Conduction across a chemical synapse is one-way, from the pre-synaptic to the post-synaptic neuron.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d9adb19d-a7a5-5fdd-81a6-293ec678e716', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Evolution', 'Artificial selection to obtain cows yielding higher milk output represents:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "stabilizing selection as it stabilizes this character in the population."}, {"id": "b", "text": "directional as it pushes the mean of the character in one direction."}, {"id": "c", "text": "disruptive as it splits the population into two, one yielding higher output and the other lower output."}, {"id": "d", "text": "stabilizing followed by disruptive as it stabilizes the population to produce higher yielding cows."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Selecting only high-yielding cows shifts the population mean toward higher milk output, which is directional selection.', 'In directional selection, individuals at one extreme of the distribution are favoured, so the mean value shifts in that direction over generations. Breeding only the highest-yielding cows steadily moves the mean milk yield upward.', '{"a": "Stabilising selection favours the average phenotype, not an extreme.", "c": "Disruptive selection favours both extremes; here only high yield is chosen.", "d": "No intermediate stabilising phase is involved."}'::jsonb, 'Natural selection can be stabilising, directional or disruptive.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('54b9731c-35a7-54e5-9543-6b51e94581b9', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Body Fluids and Circulation', 'The hepatic portal vein drains blood to liver from:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Heart"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stomach"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Kidneys"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intestine"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The hepatic portal vein carries blood from the intestine to the liver before it enters systemic circulation.', 'NCERT describes the hepatic portal system: the hepatic portal vein carries blood from the intestine to the liver. This lets the liver process absorbed nutrients before the blood reaches the heart.', '{"a": "The heart pumps blood to the liver via the hepatic artery, not the portal vein.", "b": "The textbook answer is the intestine; the portal system is described as intestine-to-liver.", "c": "Kidneys drain into the renal veins and inferior vena cava."}'::jsonb, 'A portal system connects two capillary beds; the hepatic portal and the hypophyseal portal are examples.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('c5285f55-eff7-5fb4-be58-641025661f85', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'The Living World', 'Which of the following represents order of ''Horse''?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Equidae"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perissodactyla"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Caballus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ferus"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The horse belongs to the order Perissodactyla (odd-toed ungulates).', 'Horse: genus Equus, family Equidae, order Perissodactyla, class Mammalia. Caballus and ferus are species or subspecies epithets.', '{"a": "Equidae is the family.", "c": "Caballus is a species epithet (Equus caballus).", "d": "Ferus is a species/subspecies epithet."}'::jsonb, 'Taxonomic hierarchy: Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('cd1d187e-7af4-5780-aca7-67bb31fc9a3c', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 'Alexander Von Humbolt described for the first time:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Ecological Biodiversity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Laws of limiting factor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Species area relationships"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Population Growth equation"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Humboldt first described the species–area relationship.', 'Alexander von Humboldt observed that, within a region, species richness rises with explored area up to a limit. This is expressed as log S = log C + Z log A, a rectangular hyperbola that becomes linear on a log scale.', '{"a": "Humboldt is not credited with ''ecological biodiversity'' as a concept.", "b": "The law of limiting factors is Blackman''s.", "d": "Population growth equations come from Verhulst–Pearl and others."}'::jsonb, 'Z ranges from 0.1–0.2 for small regions but is 0.6–1.2 for very large areas such as continents.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('91d77b86-a35a-5e4a-b075-0222cb8d5f00', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Biotechnology: Principles and Processes', 'DNA fragments are:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Positively charged"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Negatively charged"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Neutral"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Either positively or negatively charged depending on their size"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Phosphate groups make DNA fragments negatively charged, so they move toward the anode in electrophoresis.', 'Each nucleotide carries a phosphate group that is ionised at physiological pH. This gives DNA a net negative charge whatever its length. That is why DNA migrates toward the positive electrode in gel electrophoresis.', '{"a": "DNA is not positively charged; histones are.", "c": "Ionised phosphates make DNA charged, not neutral.", "d": "The charge sign does not depend on size; only the magnitude does."}'::jsonb, 'In agarose gels, smaller DNA fragments move farther.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('0b2c5a29-1dce-5cc6-a801-bdab6905472d', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Cell Cycle and Cell Division', 'Anaphase Promoting Complex (APC) is a protein degradation machinery necessary for proper mitosis of animal cells. If APC is defective in a human cell, which of the following is expected to occur?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Chromosomes will not condense"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chromosomes will be fragmented"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Chromosomes will not segregate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recombination of chromosome arms will occur"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Without APC, the proteins holding sister chromatids together are not degraded, so the chromatids cannot separate at anaphase.', 'APC triggers the onset of anaphase by degrading inhibitors such as securin. Separase is then released to cleave cohesin. If APC is defective, the centromeres do not split and the sister chromatids fail to segregate to opposite poles.', '{"a": "Condensation happens in prophase and does not depend on APC.", "b": "APC defects do not fragment chromosomes.", "d": "Recombination is a meiotic prophase event and is unrelated to APC."}'::jsonb, 'At anaphase the centromeres split and chromatids move to opposite poles.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a0fe78d0-47d2-56b9-a7d9-50fb21f51d78', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Animal Kingdom', 'An important characteristic that Hemichordates share with Chordates is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "absence of notochord"}, {"id": "b", "text": "ventral tubular nerve cord"}, {"id": "c", "text": "pharynx with gill slits"}, {"id": "d", "text": "pharynx without gill slits"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Like chordates, hemichordates have pharyngeal gill slits.', 'Hemichordates such as Balanoglossus have a stomochord rather than a true notochord. They do, however, possess pharyngeal gill slits, a key chordate feature. This is why they were once placed under chordates.', '{"a": "The absence of a notochord is a non-chordate trait, not one shared with chordates.", "b": "Chordates have a dorsal hollow nerve cord, not a ventral one.", "d": "Hemichordates do have gill slits."}'::jsonb, 'Chordate hallmarks: notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord and paired pharyngeal gill slits.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('55b5ecf0-c45f-50ad-8aab-cb9502295fa9', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 'The genotypes of a Husband and Wife are IᴬIᴮ and Iᴬi.
Among the blood types of their children, how many different genotypes and phenotypes are possible?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "3 genotypes ; 3 phenotypes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3 genotypes ; 4 phenotypes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4 genotypes ; 3 phenotypes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "4 genotypes ; 4 phenotypes"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'IᴬIᴮ × Iᴬi gives IᴬIᴬ, Iᴬi, IᴬIᴮ and Iᴮi: 4 genotypes but only 3 phenotypes (A, AB, B).', 'The gametes Iᴬ and Iᴮ from one parent combine with Iᴬ and i from the other. This gives IᴬIᴬ (A), Iᴬi (A), IᴬIᴮ (AB) and Iᴮi (B). The four genotypes collapse into three phenotypes because IᴬIᴬ and Iᴬi are both group A.', '{"a": "There are four distinct genotypes, not three.", "b": "Group O (ii) cannot arise, so there cannot be four phenotypes.", "d": "Group O is impossible here, so only three phenotypes occur."}'::jsonb, 'ABO blood groups show both multiple allelism and codominance (IᴬIᴮ).', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('242f11e0-d77e-5fe9-9acf-1f22df94b45d', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Human Health and Disease', 'Transplantation of tissues/organs fails often due to non-acceptance by the patient''s body. Which type of immune-response is responsible for such rejections?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Autoimmune response"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cell - mediated immune response"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hormonal immune response"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Physiological immune response"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Graft rejection is mediated by T-lymphocytes, i.e., cell-mediated immunity.', 'The body recognises the graft as ''non-self''. T-lymphocytes then mount a cell-mediated immune response that destroys it. Tissue matching and immunosuppressants are used to reduce this.', '{"a": "Autoimmunity is an attack on self tissues, not on a foreign graft.", "c": "''Hormonal'' is not an immune category; humoral (antibody) immunity is not the main cause of graft rejection.", "d": "No such immune category exists."}'::jsonb, 'Humoral immunity is mediated by B-cells and antibodies; cell-mediated immunity by T-cells.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('38537846-a55c-5072-861d-aec47bd91246', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Body Fluids and Circulation', 'Adult human RBCs are enucleate. Which of the following statement(s) is/are most appropriate explanation for this feature?
(a) They do not need to reproduce
(b) They are somatic cells
(c) They do not metabolize
(d) All their internal space is available for oxygen transport', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Only (d)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only (a)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(a), (c) and (d)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(b) and (c)"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Losing the nucleus leaves more room for haemoglobin, so the most appropriate explanation is (d) alone.', 'Mature mammalian RBCs extrude the nucleus and most organelles. This maximises space for haemoglobin, and the biconcave shape increases oxygen-carrying capacity. RBCs still metabolise anaerobically, and being somatic is not a reason to lack a nucleus.', '{"b": "Not needing to reproduce is a consequence of enucleation, not the adaptive explanation.", "c": "RBCs do metabolise, using glycolysis; most somatic cells have nuclei.", "d": "Being somatic does not explain enucleation, and RBCs are metabolically active."}'::jsonb, 'RBCs have an average life span of 120 days and are destroyed in the spleen (graveyard of RBCs).', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('3e678dbd-0555-53cc-95dd-a9465e7dd1e6', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Breathing and Exchange of Gases', 'Lungs are made up of air-filled sacs, the alveoli. They do not collapse even after forceful expiration, because of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Residual Volume"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inspiratory Reserve Volume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tidal Volume"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Expiratory Reserve Volume"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Air that stays in the lungs after a forcible expiration, the residual volume, keeps the alveoli from collapsing.', 'Residual volume (about 1100–1200 mL) is the air that remains in the lungs even after maximal expiration. It cannot be expelled, so it keeps the alveoli open and allows gas exchange to continue between breaths.', '{"b": "IRV is extra air inhaled forcibly beyond tidal inspiration.", "c": "Tidal volume is the air exchanged in normal breathing.", "d": "ERV is exhaled during forceful expiration, so it does not remain."}'::jsonb, 'FRC = ERV + RV; TLC = VC + RV.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('30ed0bfd-e9ed-5886-bf87-781008973742', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Plant Kingdom', 'Zygotic meiosis is characteristic of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Marchantia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fucus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Funaria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Chlamydomonas"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Chlamydomonas has a haplontic life cycle in which the zygote undergoes meiosis.', 'In haplontic algae such as Chlamydomonas, Volvox and Spirogyra, the only diploid stage is the zygote. It divides meiotically (zygotic meiosis). Bryophytes are haplo-diplontic, and Fucus is diplontic with gametic meiosis.', '{"a": "Marchantia is a bryophyte with a haplo-diplontic cycle and sporic meiosis.", "b": "Fucus is diplontic; meiosis occurs during gamete formation.", "c": "Funaria is a moss with sporic meiosis in the capsule."}'::jsonb, 'Ectocarpus, Polysiphonia and kelps are haplo-diplontic; Fucus is diplontic.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('144dba34-7729-5138-b21c-11ec1a5a34bb', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Structural Organisation in Animals', 'Select the correct route for the passage of sperms in male frogs:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Testes → Bidder''s canal → Kidney → Vasa efferentia → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Testes → Vasa efferentia → Kidney → Seminal Vesicle → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Testes → Vasa efferentia → Bidder''s canal → Ureter → Cloaca"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Testes → Vasa efferentia → Kidney → Bidder''s canal → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Frog sperms travel from the testes through the vasa efferentia into the kidney, then via Bidder''s canal to the urinogenital duct and cloaca.', 'NCERT states that the vasa efferentia from the testes enter the kidneys and open into Bidder''s canal. Bidder''s canal communicates with the urinogenital duct, which opens into the cloaca. So the kidney comes before Bidder''s canal.', '{"a": "The vasa efferentia arise directly from the testes, before the kidney.", "b": "The seminal vesicle is not on the described route between the kidney and the duct.", "c": "Bidder''s canal lies inside the kidney, and sperms exit via the urinogenital duct."}'::jsonb, 'In male frogs the ureter acts as a urinogenital duct; in females the ureter and oviduct open separately.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('64b0e3e0-f515-5224-b5fe-e9c54301468d', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biological Classification', 'Viroids differ from viruses in having:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "DNA molecules with protein coat"}, {"id": "b", "text": "DNA molecules without protein coat"}, {"id": "c", "text": "RNA molecules with protein coat"}, {"id": "d", "text": "RNA molecules without protein coat"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Viroids are free low-molecular-weight RNA with no protein coat.', 'T.O. Diener (1971) discovered viroids as the cause of potato spindle tuber disease. A viroid is a small circular RNA lacking the protein capsid that viruses possess.', '{"a": "Viroids lack both DNA and a protein coat.", "b": "Viroid genetic material is RNA, not DNA.", "c": "Having a protein coat is a feature of viruses."}'::jsonb, 'Prions are infectious misfolded proteins that cause BSE and CJD.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('7ffc4ddb-4680-5fb7-a74a-2957630fcf8f', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'During DNA replication, Okazaki fragments are used to elongate:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "The leading strand towards replication fork."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The lagging strand towards replication fork."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The leading strand away from replication fork."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The lagging strand away from the replication fork."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Okazaki fragments are the short pieces of the lagging strand, each synthesised 5''→3'' away from the replication fork.', 'DNA polymerase works only 5''→3''. On the template running 5''→3'' toward the fork, the new strand must be made discontinuously in short Okazaki fragments, each laid down in the direction away from the fork. DNA ligase later joins them. The leading strand is continuous and grows toward the fork.', '{"a": "The leading strand is synthesised continuously, without Okazaki fragments.", "b": "Each Okazaki fragment is polymerised away from the fork, not toward it.", "c": "The leading strand is continuous and grows toward the fork."}'::jsonb, 'DNA ligase joins Okazaki fragments on the discontinuous (lagging) strand.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('2f173d6b-2b38-5c5c-bc67-315f48858f65', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Morphology of Flowering Plants', 'Plants which produce characteristic pneumatophores and show vivipary belong to:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Mesophytes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Halophytes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Psammophytes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hydrophytes"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Mangroves are halophytes; they bear pneumatophores and show vivipary.', 'Mangroves such as Rhizophora grow in saline swamps. Their roots grow vertically upward as pneumatophores to get oxygen for respiration. Their seeds germinate while still attached to the parent plant (vivipary).', '{"a": "Mesophytes grow in moderate conditions and do not need pneumatophores.", "c": "Psammophytes grow on sand.", "d": "Hydrophytes are aquatic and use aerenchyma, not pneumatophores."}'::jsonb, 'Pneumatophores are negatively geotropic respiratory roots.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a5570097-2daf-5f8d-8112-6713f209e0d1', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Biotechnology: Principles and Processes', 'The process of separation and purification of expressed protein before marketing is called:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Upstream processing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Downstream processing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bioprocessing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Postproduction processing"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Separation and purification of the product after biosynthesis is downstream processing.', 'After the product is made in the bioreactor, it is separated, purified and formulated with preservatives. It also undergoes quality control and clinical trials before marketing. NCERT collectively calls these steps downstream processing.', '{"a": "Upstream processing is the steps before and during fermentation, such as medium preparation and inoculation.", "c": "Bioprocessing is a general term, not the specific purification stage.", "d": "This is not a standard NCERT term."}'::jsonb, 'Stirred-tank bioreactors are commonly used for large-scale culture.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('30ffbfb8-76b5-5496-a0d1-5264911a5791', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'Spliceosomes are not found in cells of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Plants"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fungi"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Animals"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bacteria"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Bacteria lack introns and therefore lack spliceosomes.', 'Spliceosomes remove introns from eukaryotic hnRNA. Prokaryotic genes are generally continuous (no introns), so bacteria do not need splicing machinery. Plants, fungi and animals are eukaryotes with split genes.', '{"a": "Plants are eukaryotes with split genes.", "b": "Fungi are eukaryotes and have spliceosomes.", "c": "Animals are eukaryotes and splice their pre-mRNA."}'::jsonb, 'In eukaryotes, hnRNA undergoes capping, tailing and splicing.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('81eadc91-6130-587d-86b0-aa3f89275d6e', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Excretory Products and their Elimination', 'Which of the following statements is correct?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "The ascending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The descending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The ascending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to water."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The descending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to electrolytes."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The ascending limb of Henle''s loop is impermeable to water but transports electrolytes.', 'The descending limb is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes, so the filtrate becomes concentrated. The ascending limb is impermeable to water and transports electrolytes out, so the filtrate becomes dilute. This difference sets up the countercurrent mechanism.', '{"b": "The descending limb is permeable to water.", "c": "The ascending limb is impermeable to water.", "d": "The descending limb is almost impermeable to electrolytes."}'::jsonb, 'The vasa recta and Henle''s loop maintain the medullary osmotic gradient (300→1200 mOsmol/L).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('89917997-eeb7-5ee4-b74d-d048edb40d93', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Ecosystem', 'Which ecosystem has the maximum biomass?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Forest ecosystem"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Grassland ecosystem"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pond ecosystem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lake ecosystem"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Forests hold the largest standing biomass because of their massive woody vegetation.', 'Forests store huge amounts of organic matter in their trees, so their standing crop per unit area is far greater than that of grasslands or aquatic systems. Aquatic ecosystems have small phytoplankton producers with a low standing biomass.', '{"b": "Grass has a much lower standing biomass than trees.", "c": "Pond producers are tiny phytoplankton with low biomass.", "d": "Lakes, like ponds, have a low standing crop of producers."}'::jsonb, 'The pyramid of biomass in the sea is inverted.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('c00a8d7c-9305-5d67-8a30-55d6d869ec3c', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'The final proof for DNA as the genetic material came from the experiments of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Griffith"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hershey and Chase"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Avery, Mcleod and McCarty"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hargobind Khorana"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Hershey and Chase (1952) gave the unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material.', 'Using bacteriophages labelled with ³²P (DNA) and ³⁵S (protein), Hershey and Chase showed that only radioactive DNA entered the bacteria and was passed on. This proved that DNA, not protein, carries the hereditary information.', '{"a": "Griffith showed transformation but did not identify the transforming principle.", "c": "Avery et al. identified DNA, but not all biologists were convinced.", "d": "Khorana worked on the genetic code and gene synthesis."}'::jsonb, 'Blender separated phage coats from bacteria; centrifugation pelleted bacteria.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('b214871a-231a-5842-8b49-14f2905915be', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Reproductive Health', 'The function of copper ions in copper releasing IUD''s is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "They suppress sperm motility and fertilising capacity of sperms."}, {"id": "b", "text": "They inhibit gametogenesis."}, {"id": "c", "text": "They make uterus unsuitable for implantation."}, {"id": "d", "text": "They inhibit ovulation."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Copper ions released by Cu-IUDs suppress sperm motility and the fertilising capacity of sperms.', 'NCERT states that IUDs increase phagocytosis of sperms in the uterus. The Cu ions they release suppress sperm motility and fertilising capacity. Making the uterus unsuitable for implantation is the role of hormone-releasing IUDs.', '{"b": "Gametogenesis is not affected by copper IUDs.", "c": "This is the action of hormone-releasing IUDs (Progestasert, LNG-20).", "d": "Inhibiting ovulation is the action of oral pills and injectables."}'::jsonb, 'Copper-releasing IUDs: CuT, Cu7, Multiload 375.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('4d7266e8-b49c-5478-a007-5006613697b4', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Plant Kingdom', 'An example of colonial alga is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Chlorella"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Volvox"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ulothrix"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Spirogyra"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Volvox is a colonial green alga.', 'Algae can be unicellular (Chlamydomonas, Chlorella), colonial (Volvox) or filamentous (Ulothrix, Spirogyra). Volvox forms spherical colonies of many flagellated cells.', '{"a": "Chlorella is unicellular.", "c": "Ulothrix is filamentous.", "d": "Spirogyra is filamentous."}'::jsonb, 'Chlorella is used as a protein-rich food supplement even by space travellers.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('9b79e008-2b02-51dc-8662-f7278f1765c2', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Morphology of Flowering Plants', 'Root hairs develop from the region of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Maturation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Elongation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Root cap"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Meristematic activity"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Root hairs arise from epidermal cells in the region of maturation.', 'Behind the elongation zone, cells differentiate and mature. Some epidermal cells of this region form thread-like root hairs that absorb water and minerals.', '{"b": "Cells in the elongation zone enlarge but do not yet form hairs.", "c": "The root cap protects the apex.", "d": "The meristematic zone consists of small dividing cells."}'::jsonb, 'Root regions from tip: root cap, meristematic zone, elongation zone, maturation zone.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('0be130bc-a524-5b6a-b0a7-d11f5fcc428c', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Chemical Coordination and Integration', 'Hypersecretion of Growth Hormone in adults does not cause further increase in height, because:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Growth Hormone becomes inactive in adults."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Epiphyseal plates close after adolescence."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bones loose their sensitivity to Growth Hormone in adults."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Muscle fibres do not grow in size after birth."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Once the epiphyseal growth plates fuse after adolescence, long bones cannot lengthen, so excess GH causes acromegaly rather than gigantism.', 'Longitudinal bone growth occurs at the cartilaginous epiphyseal plates. These ossify after puberty. Excess GH in adults then thickens bones of the face, hands and feet (acromegaly) but cannot increase height.', '{"a": "GH remains active in adults; it causes acromegaly.", "c": "Bones still respond to GH by thickening.", "d": "Muscle fibres can grow in size (hypertrophy); this is not the reason."}'::jsonb, 'Excess GH in childhood causes gigantism; deficiency causes pituitary dwarfism.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('e8131c7a-5a97-51f8-9ec1-8125199f0462', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 'Which of the following in sewage treatment removes suspended solids?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Tertiary treatment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Secondary treatment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Primary treatment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sludge treatment"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Primary treatment physically removes floating debris and suspended solids by filtration and sedimentation.', 'In primary treatment, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration, and grit and soil settle out. The settled solids form primary sludge and the supernatant becomes the effluent. Secondary (biological) treatment then reduces the BOD using microbes.', '{"a": "Tertiary treatment is not part of the NCERT scheme; it refers to advanced chemical polishing.", "b": "Secondary treatment is biological and reduces organic matter (BOD).", "d": "Sludge treatment, i.e. anaerobic digestion of sludge, produces biogas."}'::jsonb, 'Flocs are masses of bacteria and fungal filaments formed in the aeration tank.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('857e3592-61a9-5089-a5ca-2eb7c8f22fdb', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Plant Kingdom', 'Select the mismatch:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Pinus - Dioecious"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cycas - Dioecious"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Salvinia - Heterosporous"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Equisetum - Homosporous"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Pinus is monoecious, bearing male and female cones on the same plant.', 'Pinus bears male and female strobili on the same tree (monoecious). Cycas is dioecious. Salvinia is a heterosporous pteridophyte, while Equisetum is homosporous.', '{"b": "Cycas is correctly dioecious.", "c": "Salvinia is correctly heterosporous.", "d": "Equisetum is correctly homosporous."}'::jsonb, 'Heterospory in Selaginella and Salvinia is a precursor to the seed habit.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('8124a3cd-69c1-5446-aeb1-5f219bfc4215', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Biotechnology: Principles and Processes', 'What is the criterion for DNA fragments movement on agarose gel during gel electrophoresis?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "The larger the fragment size, the farther it moves"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Positively charged fragments move to farther end"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Negatively charged fragments do not move"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Agarose acts as a sieve, so smaller DNA fragments move farther.', 'All DNA fragments are negatively charged and move toward the anode. The agarose matrix retards larger fragments more, so separation is by size: smaller fragments travel farther from the well.', '{"a": "This is the reverse; larger fragments move less.", "c": "DNA fragments are negatively charged.", "d": "Negatively charged DNA does move, toward the anode."}'::jsonb, 'Separated DNA is stained with ethidium bromide and seen as orange bands under UV.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('2c3d2145-0dbf-56b9-bc1b-ce017b7d38ca', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Morphology of Flowering Plants', 'In Bougainvillea thorns are the modifications of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Stipules"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Adventitious root"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leaf"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Bougainvillea thorns are modified axillary buds, i.e., stem modifications.', 'Thorns arise from the axillary position, where buds normally occur. This shows they are modified stems. They are hard, woody and pointed, and they protect against grazing animals.', '{"a": "Stipule spines occur in plants such as Zizyphus.", "b": "Thorns are not root structures.", "d": "Leaf spines occur in cactus/Opuntia, not Bougainvillea."}'::jsonb, 'Stem tendrils (cucumber, grapevine) and thorns (Citrus, Bougainvillea) arise from axillary buds.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('18c93047-f570-503d-8cdc-28ab819773f1', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'The association of histone H1 with a nucleosome indicates:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Transcription is occurring."}, {"id": "b", "text": "DNA replication is occurring."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The DNA is condensed into a Chromatin Fibre."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The DNA double helix is exposed."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'H1 binding to linker DNA packs nucleosomes into the higher-order chromatin fibre.', 'The histone octamer forms the nucleosome core, and H1 binds the linker DNA. This allows nucleosomes to coil into the 30 nm chromatin fibre, i.e. condensed, transcriptionally less active chromatin.', '{"a": "Condensation by H1 tends to repress transcription.", "b": "Replication needs the chromatin to unwind, not to condense.", "d": "H1 binding hides the DNA rather than exposing it."}'::jsonb, 'A nucleosome contains about 200 bp of DNA wrapped around a histone octamer.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('c3520045-5238-5a92-af17-6c87d1c155e7', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Chemical Coordination and Integration', 'A temporary endocrine gland in the human body is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Pineal gland"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Corpus cardiacum"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Corpus luteum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Corpus allatum"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The corpus luteum forms after ovulation and degenerates if no pregnancy occurs, so it is a temporary endocrine gland.', 'After ovulation the ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone. Without fertilisation it degenerates into the corpus albicans, so it is temporary. Corpus cardiacum and corpus allatum are insect endocrine glands.', '{"a": "The pineal gland is a permanent endocrine gland.", "b": "Corpus cardiacum is an insect gland, not a human one.", "d": "Corpus allatum is an insect gland that secretes juvenile hormone."}'::jsonb, 'The placenta is another temporary endocrine organ (hCG, hPL).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('47309370-d450-5699-8873-070b5e5142db', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 'Select the mismatch:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Frankia - Alnus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rhodospirillum - Mycorrhiza"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Anabaena - Nitrogen fixer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rhizobium - Alfalfa"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Rhodospirillum is a free-living anaerobic nitrogen-fixing bacterium, not a mycorrhizal partner.', 'Mycorrhiza is an association between fungi (e.g., Glomus) and plant roots. Rhodospirillum is a free-living, photosynthetic, nitrogen-fixing bacterium. Frankia nodulates Alnus, Rhizobium nodulates legumes such as alfalfa, and Anabaena fixes nitrogen.', '{"a": "Frankia does form root nodules in Alnus.", "c": "Anabaena is a nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium.", "d": "Rhizobium does nodulate alfalfa, a legume."}'::jsonb, 'Glomus forms mycorrhiza that helps plants absorb phosphorus.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('6a37e599-e67b-516e-beab-b51363da4f2a', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Human Reproduction', 'GnRH, a hypothalamic hormone, needed in reproduction, acts on:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "anterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and oxytocin."}, {"id": "b", "text": "anterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and FSH."}, {"id": "c", "text": "posterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of oxytocin and FSH."}, {"id": "d", "text": "posterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and relaxin."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'GnRH stimulates the anterior pituitary to release the gonadotropins LH and FSH.', 'GnRH from the hypothalamus reaches the adenohypophysis via the hypophyseal portal system. There it triggers the release of LH and FSH, which act on the gonads. Oxytocin is stored in the posterior pituitary and is not controlled by GnRH.', '{"a": "Oxytocin is a posterior pituitary hormone, not released by GnRH.", "c": "GnRH does not act on the posterior pituitary.", "d": "Relaxin comes from the ovary/placenta, and GnRH does not act on the posterior pituitary."}'::jsonb, 'In males, LH stimulates Leydig cells (androgens) and FSH acts on Sertoli cells.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('7c52b642-234b-5ceb-8bc1-744577eca4ce', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Biotechnology: Principles and Processes', 'A gene whose expression helps to identify transformed cell is known as:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Selectable marker"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vector"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Plasmid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Structural gene"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'A selectable marker gene lets transformants be identified and selected.', 'Selectable markers such as antibiotic resistance genes (ampʳ, tetʳ) let only the transformed cells survive on selective media. Insertional inactivation of lacZ or antibiotic genes then helps to pick out recombinants.', '{"b": "A vector is the vehicle that carries DNA.", "c": "A plasmid is a type of vector.", "d": "A structural gene codes for a protein product."}'::jsonb, 'Blue-white screening: recombinants with inactivated lacZ give white colonies.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('eeeae7e1-2ac6-58b5-8a1a-646f2234de4a', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Ecosystem', 'Presence of plants arranged into well defined vertical layers depending on their height can be seen best in:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Tropical Savannah"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tropical Rain Forest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Grassland"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Temperate Forest"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Vertical stratification is most pronounced in tropical rain forests.', 'Stratification is the vertical distribution of species at different levels. Tropical rain forests show the most distinct layering: emergent trees, canopy, understory, shrubs and herbs. This is a result of their high productivity and diversity.', '{"a": "Savannahs have scattered trees and little layering.", "c": "Grasslands have mainly one herbaceous layer.", "d": "Temperate forests show layering, but less than rain forests."}'::jsonb, 'Stratification is a key structural feature of an ecosystem.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('86cadd7b-f06f-521c-a302-4fd885313b75', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 'Functional megaspore in an angiosperm develops into:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Ovule"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Endosperm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Embryo sac"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Embryo"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The functional megaspore undergoes three free-nuclear mitoses to form the embryo sac (female gametophyte).', 'Of the four megaspores formed by meiosis of the megaspore mother cell, usually one is functional. Its nucleus divides mitotically three times to give the 8-nucleate, 7-celled embryo sac. Thus the megaspore gives rise to the female gametophyte.', '{"a": "The ovule is the megasporangium that contains the megaspore, not its product.", "b": "Endosperm forms from the primary endosperm nucleus after triple fusion.", "d": "The embryo develops from the zygote."}'::jsonb, 'Monosporic development: one functional megaspore forms the 8-nucleate, 7-celled embryo sac (Polygonum type).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('694ed68b-c15c-5b94-a14d-dcc7cb899fb7', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'DNA replication in bacteria occurs:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "During S phase"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Within nucleolus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prior to fission"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Just before transcription"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Bacteria replicate their circular DNA before dividing by binary fission.', 'Prokaryotes lack a defined cell cycle with S phase and lack a nucleus or nucleolus. The bacterial chromosome is copied before the cell divides by binary fission so that each daughter cell gets one copy.', '{"a": "S phase is a term for the eukaryotic cell cycle.", "b": "Bacteria have no nucleolus.", "d": "Replication is not coupled to each transcription event."}'::jsonb, 'Bacterial replication starts at a single origin (ori) on the circular chromosome.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('b12f0b73-66cc-5ef2-aa26-5ec976e17f1c', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Animal Kingdom', 'Which among these is the correct combination of aquatic mammals?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Seals, Dolphins, Sharks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dolphins, Seals, Trygon"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Whales, Dolphins, Seals"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Trygon, Whales, Seals"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Whales, dolphins and seals are all aquatic mammals.', 'Sharks and Trygon (stingray) are cartilaginous fishes (Chondrichthyes). Whales, dolphins and seals are mammals adapted to aquatic life; they breathe with lungs and suckle their young.', '{"a": "Sharks are cartilaginous fish.", "b": "Trygon is a stingray, a cartilaginous fish.", "d": "Trygon is a cartilaginous fish."}'::jsonb, 'Trygon has a poison sting; Torpedo has electric organs. Both are Chondrichthyes.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d0a7a9ca-ad7d-5fe9-ab21-73750a10e15c', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Morphology of Flowering Plants', 'Coconut fruit is a:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Drupe"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Berry"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nut"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Capsule"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Coconut is a drupe with a fibrous mesocarp and a hard stony endocarp.', 'A drupe develops from a monocarpellary superior ovary and is one-seeded. In coconut the mesocarp is fibrous and the endocarp is hard and stony, like the mango.', '{"b": "A berry has a fleshy pericarp and usually many seeds.", "c": "In a nut the whole pericarp becomes hard; coconut has a fibrous mesocarp.", "d": "A capsule is a dry dehiscent fruit."}'::jsonb, 'Drupes: mango (edible fleshy mesocarp) and coconut (edible endosperm, fibrous mesocarp).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('fad7c74f-2e7a-5bd7-8ecb-d7a762cb00c1', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 'Double fertilization is exhibited by:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Gymnosperms"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Algae"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fungi"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Angiosperms"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Double fertilization (syngamy plus triple fusion) is unique to angiosperms.', 'In angiosperms one male gamete fuses with the egg to form the zygote, and the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the triploid primary endosperm nucleus. These two fusions in one embryo sac make up double fertilization.', '{"a": "Gymnosperms have haploid endosperm formed before fertilization; no triple fusion.", "b": "Algae show simple syngamy.", "c": "Fungi show plasmogamy and karyogamy, not double fertilization."}'::jsonb, 'Syngamy + triple fusion = double fertilization; endosperm in angiosperms is 3n.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('709dd5af-9086-5946-8817-92b829753988', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Cell: The Unit of Life', 'Which of the following components provides sticky character to the bacterial cell?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Cell wall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Nuclear membrane"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Plasma membrane"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Glycocalyx"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The glycocalyx (slime layer or capsule) makes the bacterial cell sticky.', 'The bacterial cell envelope has three layers: glycocalyx outside, then the cell wall, then the plasma membrane. The glycocalyx may be a loose slime layer or a thick, tough capsule, and it helps bacteria adhere to surfaces.', '{"a": "The cell wall gives shape and prevents bursting.", "b": "Bacteria lack a nuclear membrane.", "c": "The plasma membrane is selectively permeable, not sticky."}'::jsonb, 'Glycocalyx: loose sheath = slime layer; thick tough = capsule.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ec3578d1-39ee-5be3-b732-9402c9e3a601', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Plant Kingdom', 'Life cycle of Ectocarpus and Fucus respectively are:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Haplontic, Diplontic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Diplontic, Haplodiplontic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Haplodiplontic, Diplontic"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Haplodiplontic, Haplontic"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Ectocarpus is haplodiplontic and Fucus is diplontic.', 'Ectocarpus, Polysiphonia and kelps show alternation of multicellular haploid and diploid phases (haplodiplontic). Fucus, like seed plants, has a dominant diploid sporophyte with gametes as the only haploid stage (diplontic).', '{"a": "Ectocarpus is not haplontic.", "b": "The order is reversed.", "d": "Fucus is diplontic, not haplontic."}'::jsonb, 'Haplontic: Volvox, Spirogyra, Chlamydomonas; diplontic: Fucus; haplodiplontic: bryophytes, pteridophytes, Ectocarpus, Polysiphonia, kelps.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('13dcbd4d-e162-5729-917b-fa2f9b40c3a9', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 'Which one of the following is related to Ex-situ conservation of threatened animals and plants?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Wildlife Safari parks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Biodiversity hot spots"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Amazon rainforest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Himalayan region"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Wildlife safari parks protect organisms outside their natural habitat, which is ex-situ conservation.', 'Ex-situ conservation removes threatened species from their natural habitat and keeps them in special settings such as zoos, botanical gardens and wildlife safari parks. Hotspots and natural forests are protected in-situ.', '{"b": "Hotspots are a strategy for in-situ conservation.", "c": "Amazon rainforest is a natural habitat (in-situ).", "d": "The Himalayan region is a natural habitat (in-situ)."}'::jsonb, 'Ex-situ: zoos, botanical gardens, safari parks, seed banks, cryopreservation.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('90d0df2d-3e61-5b22-8d73-d789ba302cdc', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Neural Control and Coordination', 'Good vision depends on adequate intake of carotene-rich food.
Select the best option from the following statements.
(a) Vitamin A derivatives are formed from carotene.
(b) The photopigments are embedded in the membrane discs of the inner segment.
(c) Retinal is a derivative of Vitamin A.
(d) Retinal is a light absorbing part of all the visual photopigments.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(a) and (b)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "(a), (c) and (d)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(a) and (c)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(b), (c) and (d)"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Carotene gives vitamin A, retinal is derived from vitamin A, and retinal is the light-absorbing part of all photopigments; the photopigments lie in the outer segment.', 'Beta-carotene is converted to vitamin A, and retinal (retinaldehyde) is a vitamin A derivative. Retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin and the cone photopigments, so it absorbs light in all of them. Statement (b) is wrong because the pigment-bearing membrane discs are in the outer segment of rods and cones.', '{"a": "Includes the wrong statement (b).", "c": "Leaves out the correct statement (d).", "d": "Includes (b) and leaves out (a)."}'::jsonb, 'Rhodopsin = opsin + retinal; light makes retinal change shape and opsin change structure.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ced98501-445f-5ce9-9ea6-e0bc2b27f2bb', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 'Thalassemia and sickle cell anemia are caused due to a problem in globin molecule synthesis. Select the correct statement.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Both are due to a qualitative defect in globin chain synthesis."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both are due to a quantitative defect in globin chain synthesis."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thalassemia is due to less synthesis of globin molecules."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sickle cell anemia is due to a quantitative problem of globin molecules."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Thalassemia is a quantitative defect (too few globin chains); sickle cell anaemia is a qualitative defect.', 'In thalassemia, mutation or deletion reduces the rate of synthesis of α or β globin chains, so too little globin is made. In sickle cell anaemia, one amino acid substitution (Glu to Val at position 6 of the β chain) makes a faulty globin, which is a qualitative problem.', '{"a": "Thalassemia is quantitative, not qualitative.", "b": "Sickle cell anaemia is qualitative.", "d": "Sickle cell anaemia is a qualitative defect."}'::jsonb, 'Sickle cell: GAG→GUG, Glu→Val at β6; thalassemia: reduced globin synthesis.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('568a11cd-0f34-56ed-960d-e12ee972d8a5', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following are not polymeric?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Nucleic acids"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Proteins"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Polysaccharides"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lipids"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Lipids are not polymers.', 'Nucleic acids (polynucleotides), proteins (polypeptides) and polysaccharides are macromolecules built from repeating monomers. Lipids have small molecular weights; they appear in the acid-insoluble fraction only because they form membrane vesicles.', '{"a": "Nucleic acids are polymers of nucleotides.", "b": "Proteins are polymers of amino acids.", "c": "Polysaccharides are polymers of monosaccharides."}'::jsonb, 'Lipids are not strictly macromolecules even though they appear in the acid-insoluble pool.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('175135f6-30d6-57c3-a6c4-89faf5ce2b45', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 'A disease caused by an autosomal primary non-disjunction is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Down''s Syndrome"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Klinefelter''s Syndrome"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Turner''s Syndrome"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sickle Cell Anemia"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Down''s syndrome (trisomy 21) is caused by non-disjunction of an autosome.', 'Down''s syndrome results from an extra copy of chromosome 21, an autosome, due to non-disjunction during meiosis. Klinefelter''s (XXY) and Turner''s (XO) involve sex chromosomes, and sickle cell anaemia is a point mutation.', '{"b": "Klinefelter''s is a sex-chromosome aneuploidy (47, XXY).", "c": "Turner''s is a sex-chromosome aneuploidy (45, XO).", "d": "Sickle cell anaemia is a Mendelian (point mutation) disorder."}'::jsonb, 'Down''s syndrome: trisomy 21, described by Langdon Down (1866).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d677305b-222a-5d06-b780-173711e36b45', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants', 'With reference to factors affecting the rate of photosynthesis, which of the following statements is not correct?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Light saturation for CO₂ fixation occurs at 10% of full sunlight"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Increasing atmospheric CO₂ concentration up to 0.05% can enhance CO₂ fixation rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "C₃ plants respond to higher temperatures with enhanced photosynthesis while C₄ plants have much lower temperature optimum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tomato is a greenhouse crop which can be grown in CO₂-enriched atmosphere for higher yield"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'C₄ plants, not C₃ plants, have the higher temperature optimum, so statement (3) is wrong.', 'C₄ plants respond to higher temperatures and show a higher rate of photosynthesis, while C₃ plants have a much lower temperature optimum. Statement (3) reverses this. The other statements match NCERT: light saturates at about 10% of full sunlight, CO₂ up to 0.05% raises fixation, and tomato is grown in CO₂-enriched greenhouses.', '{"a": "Correct NCERT fact, so not the answer.", "b": "Correct; beyond 0.05% CO₂ can become damaging over long periods.", "d": "Correct; greenhouse crops like tomato and bell pepper benefit from CO₂ enrichment."}'::jsonb, 'C₄ plants have a higher temperature optimum and are not saturated by CO₂ beyond 360 μL L⁻¹ the way C₃ plants are.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('eac218f3-b158-53aa-a69b-1f37f0b28ab5', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Plant Growth and Development', 'Fruit and leaf drop at early stages can be prevented by the application of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Cytokinins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ethylene"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Auxins"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gibberellic acid"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Auxins prevent premature fruit and leaf drop.', 'Auxins such as NAA and 2,4-D are sprayed to prevent early fruit and leaf drop, while they promote abscission of older, mature leaves and fruits. Ethylene promotes abscission instead.', '{"a": "Cytokinins delay senescence but are not the standard anti-drop spray.", "b": "Ethylene promotes abscission.", "d": "Gibberellins mainly cause stem elongation, bolting and fruit enlargement."}'::jsonb, 'Auxins: prevent premature drop, promote apical dominance, parthenocarpy and rooting of cuttings.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('4f6e1d26-6e27-574d-9ea5-0e36765695ff', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 'The region of Biosphere Reserve which is legally protected and where no human activity is allowed is known as:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Core zone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Buffer zone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transition zone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restoration zone"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The core zone is legally protected and free of human activity.', 'A biosphere reserve has a central core zone with strict protection, a surrounding buffer zone that allows limited research and education, and an outer transition zone for human settlement and use.', '{"b": "The buffer zone allows limited human activity.", "c": "The transition zone allows settlement and cultivation.", "d": "A restoration zone is not the legally protected core."}'::jsonb, 'India has 18 biosphere reserves; the core zone has no human interference.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('95e72957-0080-5d8b-91fa-236fce098607', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Animal Kingdom', 'In case of poriferans, the spongocoel is lined with flagellated cells called:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "ostia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "oscula"}, {"id": "c", "text": "choanocytes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "mesenchymal cells"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Choanocytes (collar cells) line the spongocoel and canals of sponges.', 'Sponges have a water canal system: water enters through ostia into the spongocoel and leaves through the osculum. The spongocoel and canals are lined by flagellated choanocytes, which drive the water current.', '{"a": "Ostia are the pores where water enters.", "b": "The osculum is the opening where water exits.", "d": "Mesenchymal cells lie in the matrix, not lining the cavity."}'::jsonb, 'Water path in sponges: ostia → spongocoel → osculum; choanocytes create the flow.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('fee456c4-e8a6-5654-a717-8b075a8371a6', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Excretory Products and their Elimination', 'A decrease in blood pressure/volume will not cause the release of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Renin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Atrial Natriuretic Factor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aldosterone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "ADH"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'ANF is released when blood pressure or volume rises, not when it falls.', 'A fall in blood pressure or volume activates the JG cells to release renin, which leads to angiotensin II and aldosterone, and also triggers ADH release. The atria release ANF only when they are stretched by increased volume or pressure, and ANF lowers blood pressure.', '{"a": "Renin is released by JG cells when GFR or blood pressure falls.", "c": "Aldosterone rises through angiotensin II when pressure falls.", "d": "ADH rises when blood volume falls."}'::jsonb, 'ANF causes vasodilation and acts as a check on the renin–angiotensin mechanism.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ea9c38db-f7a1-5702-8e71-4c854fa28b19', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 'A dioecious flowering plant prevents both:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Autogamy and xenogamy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Autogamy and geitonogamy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Geitonogamy and xenogamy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cleistogamy and xenogamy"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Dioecy (separate male and female plants) rules out both autogamy and geitonogamy.', 'In a dioecious plant, each plant has flowers of only one sex. Pollen cannot reach the same flower (autogamy) or another flower of the same plant (geitonogamy), so only xenogamy is possible.', '{"a": "Xenogamy is the only mode possible in dioecious plants.", "c": "Xenogamy is not prevented.", "d": "Xenogamy is not prevented."}'::jsonb, 'Monoecy (castor, maize) prevents autogamy but not geitonogamy; dioecy (papaya) prevents both.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('005be9f0-8471-5508-8a4e-4b3ba6aa3808', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Biotechnology: Principles and Processes', 'The DNA fragments separated on an agarose gel can be visualised after staining with:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Bromophenol blue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acetocarmine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aniline blue"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ethidium bromide"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Ethidium bromide stains DNA bands, which glow orange under UV light.', 'After gel electrophoresis, the gel is stained with ethidium bromide, which intercalates into DNA. The bands then appear bright orange under UV light and can be cut out and eluted.', '{"a": "Bromophenol blue is a tracking dye that shows how far the run has gone.", "b": "Acetocarmine stains chromosomes.", "c": "Aniline blue is used for callose and pollen tubes, not gel DNA."}'::jsonb, 'Smaller DNA fragments move farther through the agarose sieve toward the anode.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d5e9b252-2d6c-57e9-b255-0389d06c4fc7', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Respiration in Plants', 'Which statement is wrong for Krebs'' cycle?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "There are three points in the cycle where NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH + H⁺"}, {"id": "b", "text": "There is one point in the cycle where FAD⁺ is reduced to FADH₂"}, {"id": "c", "text": "During conversion of succinyl CoA to succinic acid, a molecule of GTP is synthesised"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The cycle starts with condensation of acetyl group (acetyl CoA) with pyruvic acid to yield citric acid"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Acetyl CoA condenses with oxaloacetic acid, not pyruvic acid, to form citric acid.', 'The TCA cycle starts when the acetyl group of acetyl CoA combines with oxaloacetic acid (OAA) and water to give citric acid, catalysed by citrate synthase. Each turn reduces three NAD⁺ and one FAD and makes one GTP by substrate-level phosphorylation at the succinyl CoA step, so statements 1–3 are correct.', '{"a": "Correct: three NADH per turn.", "b": "Correct: one FADH₂ at succinate → fumarate.", "c": "Correct: substrate-level phosphorylation gives GTP."}'::jsonb, 'Per pyruvate in the Krebs cycle: 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 GTP, 2 CO₂ (plus 1 NADH from pyruvate → acetyl CoA).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('b5c7abb9-8989-5945-a9c6-08f57e804323', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Organisms and Populations', 'Mycorrhizae are the example of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Fungistasis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Amensalism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Antibiosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mutualism"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Mycorrhiza is a mutualistic association between fungi and plant roots.', 'The fungus absorbs phosphorus and other nutrients from the soil for the plant, and the plant gives the fungus carbohydrates. Both partners benefit, so the relationship is mutualism.', '{"a": "Fungistasis means inhibition of fungal growth.", "b": "In amensalism one is harmed and the other is unaffected.", "c": "Antibiosis is inhibition by antibiotic substances."}'::jsonb, 'Mutualism examples: lichens, mycorrhizae, fig–wasp, Ophrys–bee (pseudocopulation).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('cc56bd1c-0ff2-5587-b303-8deb0525f5f5', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Locomotion and Movement', 'The pivot joint between atlas and axis is a type of:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "fibrous joint"}, {"id": "b", "text": "cartilaginous joint"}, {"id": "c", "text": "synovial joint"}, {"id": "d", "text": "saddle joint"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The atlas–axis pivot joint is a synovial joint.', 'Synovial joints have a fluid-filled synovial cavity and allow considerable movement. They include ball and socket, hinge, pivot (atlas–axis), gliding and saddle types. The atlanto-axial joint is a pivot joint.', '{"a": "Fibrous joints (skull sutures) allow no movement.", "b": "Cartilaginous joints join adjacent vertebrae bodies with limited movement.", "d": "A saddle joint is the carpal–metacarpal joint of the thumb, not atlas–axis."}'::jsonb, 'Pivot joint: between atlas and axis; saddle joint: carpal and metacarpal of thumb.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('b9b639eb-ce03-535c-bc2b-ec2e9b90ba7b', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 'Which of the following is correctly matched for the product produced by them?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Acetobacter aceti : Antibiotics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Methanobacterium : Lactic acid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Penicillium notatum : Acetic acid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae : Ethanol"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Saccharomyces cerevisiae (brewer''s yeast) is used to produce ethanol.', 'Yeast ferments sugars to ethanol in industrial production and in brewing. Acetobacter aceti makes acetic acid, Penicillium notatum makes penicillin, and Methanobacterium makes methane (biogas).', '{"a": "Acetobacter aceti produces acetic acid.", "b": "Methanobacterium produces methane; Lactobacillus makes lactic acid.", "c": "Penicillium notatum produces penicillin."}'::jsonb, 'Organic acids: Aspergillus niger (citric), Acetobacter aceti (acetic), Clostridium butylicum (butyric), Lactobacillus (lactic).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('e92440dc-da8c-55d6-a49f-a06fa00b7156', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Body Fluids and Circulation', 'Frog''s heart when taken out of the body continues to beat for sometime.
Select the best option from the following statements.
(a) Frog is a poikilotherm.
(b) Frog does not have any coronary circulation.
(c) Heart is "myogenic" in nature.
(d) Heart is autoexcitable.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Only (c)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only (d)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(a) and (b)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(c) and (d)"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The heart keeps beating outside the body because it is myogenic and autoexcitable.', 'The beat starts in specialised cardiac muscle (the pacemaker or SA node) and does not need nervous input. Because the heart generates its own impulses, it is both myogenic and autoexcitable, so it keeps contracting for some time after removal. Being cold-blooded is not the cause.', '{"a": "Each of (c) and (d) alone is incomplete.", "b": "Each of (c) and (d) alone is incomplete.", "c": "Poikilothermy and coronary circulation do not explain the beat."}'::jsonb, 'The SA node is the pacemaker; it generates 70–75 action potentials per minute in humans.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('dba4c4f3-ff70-5e0b-9851-2f4888fd473c', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Neural Control and Coordination', 'Myelin sheath is produced by:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Schwann Cells and Oligodendrocytes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Astrocytes and Schwann Cells"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oligodendrocytes and Osteoclasts"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoclasts and Astrocytes"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Schwann cells (PNS) and oligodendrocytes (CNS) make myelin.', 'In peripheral nerves, Schwann cells wrap around axons to form the myelin sheath. In the brain and spinal cord, oligodendrocytes do the same. Astrocytes support neurons and osteoclasts resorb bone.', '{"b": "Astrocytes do not form myelin.", "c": "Osteoclasts are bone-resorbing cells.", "d": "Neither osteoclasts nor astrocytes form myelin."}'::jsonb, 'Gaps between adjacent myelin sheaths are the nodes of Ranvier, which allow saltatory conduction.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('6ff900f6-8eec-5dff-ab9f-5d56bd6a1e4a', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Human Reproduction', 'Capacitation occurs in:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Rete testis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Epididymis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Vas deferens"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Female Reproductive tract"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Sperm undergo capacitation inside the female reproductive tract.', 'Capacitation is the final maturation of sperm that makes them able to fertilise the egg. It involves changes in the sperm membrane and happens in the female reproductive tract (uterus and oviduct) after ejaculation.', '{"a": "The rete testis only carries sperm from the seminiferous tubules.", "b": "The epididymis stores sperm and gives them motility, but not capacitation.", "c": "The vas deferens only transports sperm."}'::jsonb, 'Capacitation lasts about 5–6 hours in the female tract.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('0b5bc1e5-4f65-5218-ac3f-f61ebfb166b8', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Morphology of Flowering Plants', 'The morphological nature of the edible part of coconut is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Perisperm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cotyledon"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Endosperm"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pericarp"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The edible white kernel and coconut water are the endosperm.', 'In coconut, the primary endosperm nucleus first divides to form free-nuclear endosperm (the coconut water). The surrounding white kernel is cellular endosperm. Both are endosperm tissue.', '{"a": "Perisperm is persistent nucellus, as in black pepper.", "b": "The cotyledon is not the edible part of coconut.", "d": "The pericarp is the husk and shell."}'::jsonb, 'Coconut water = free-nuclear endosperm; white kernel = cellular endosperm.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a93faf82-7c1c-569f-b9d2-36f3ea724f60', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Anatomy of Flowering Plants', 'Which of the following is made up of dead cells?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Xylem parenchyma"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collenchyma"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Phellem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Phloem"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Phellem (cork) is made of dead, suberised cells.', 'Cork cambium cuts off cells outward that form phellem. Suberin is deposited in their walls, making them impervious, and the cells die. Xylem parenchyma, collenchyma and phloem cells are living.', '{"a": "Xylem parenchyma is living.", "b": "Collenchyma is living with pectin-thickened corners.", "d": "Phloem sieve tubes, companion cells and parenchyma are living (only phloem fibres are dead)."}'::jsonb, 'Periderm = phellogen + phellem (cork) + phelloderm.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('2d8d7732-89ad-56bb-8591-6e1e85073e4d', 'neet_ug', 'Zoology', 'Reproductive Health', 'In case of a couple where the male is having a very low sperm count, which technique will be suitable for fertilisation?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Intrauterine transfer"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Gamete intracytoplasmic fallopian transfer"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Artificial Insemination"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intracytoplasmic sperm injection"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'With a very low sperm count, ICSI injects a sperm directly into the ovum.', 'Intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) needs only a single sperm, which is injected into the ovum''s cytoplasm in the laboratory to form an embryo. This makes it suitable when the male partner has very few sperm. Artificial insemination is used for less severe low counts and still needs many sperm.', '{"a": "IUT transfers an embryo with more than 8 blastomeres and is not about the sperm problem.", "b": "This is not a standard ART name; GIFT transfers ova.", "c": "AI needs enough sperm; with very low counts ICSI is better."}'::jsonb, 'ICSI is a specialised lab procedure in which a sperm is injected directly into the ovum.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('2e69e07f-f187-5c73-a306-ca1d29f6badb', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 'Which of the following RNAs should be most abundant in animal cell?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "r-RNA"}, {"id": "b", "text": "t-RNA"}, {"id": "c", "text": "m-RNA"}, {"id": "d", "text": "mi-RNA"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'rRNA is the most abundant RNA in a cell (about 80%).', 'Ribosomes are present in huge numbers, and rRNA makes up a large part of their mass. So rRNA forms about 80% of total cellular RNA, while tRNA and mRNA make up smaller fractions.', '{"b": "tRNA is about 10–15%.", "c": "mRNA is only about 5%.", "d": "miRNAs are small regulatory RNAs present in minute amounts."}'::jsonb, 'The 23S rRNA in bacteria acts as a ribozyme for peptide bond formation.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a45f3368-c96a-5636-baa4-a91948ce088d', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Anatomy of Flowering Plants', 'The vascular cambium normally gives rise to:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Phelloderm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Primary phloem"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Secondary xylem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Periderm"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Vascular cambium produces secondary xylem inward and secondary phloem outward.', 'During secondary growth, the vascular cambium is more active on the inner side, so it produces more secondary xylem than secondary phloem. Phelloderm and periderm come from cork cambium, and primary phloem comes from procambium.', '{"a": "Phelloderm is produced by the cork cambium.", "b": "Primary phloem comes from the procambium of apical meristems.", "d": "Periderm comes from the cork cambium."}'::jsonb, 'Cambium makes more xylem than phloem, so wood accumulates as annual rings.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('9a2bf83d-7662-554e-9957-34a4c77b3822', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Cell Cycle and Cell Division', 'Which of the following options gives the correct sequence of events during mitosis?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "condensation → nuclear membrane disassembly → crossing over → segregation → telophase"}, {"id": "b", "text": "condensation → nuclear membrane disassembly → arrangement at equator → centromere division → segregation → telophase"}, {"id": "c", "text": "condensation → crossing over → nuclear membrane disassembly → segregation → telophase"}, {"id": "d", "text": "condensation → arrangement at equator → centromere division → segregation → telophase"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Mitosis proceeds: condensation, nuclear envelope breakdown, metaphase alignment, centromere split, segregation, telophase.', 'In prophase chromatin condenses, and by its end the nuclear envelope breaks down. In metaphase chromosomes line up at the equator. In anaphase centromeres split and chromatids segregate to the poles, followed by telophase. Crossing over occurs only in meiosis.', '{"a": "Crossing over does not occur in mitosis.", "c": "Crossing over does not occur in mitosis.", "d": "Leaves out nuclear membrane disassembly before metaphase alignment."}'::jsonb, 'Anaphase: centromeres split and chromatids move to opposite poles.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f97b5b92-9549-5485-9a78-6129cd4aef50', 'neet_ug', 'Botany', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 'Attractants and rewards are required for:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Anemophily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Entomophily"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hydrophily"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cleistogamy"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Insect-pollinated flowers need attractants (colour, scent) and rewards (nectar, pollen).', 'Insects visit flowers that attract them with bright colours, fragrance and nectar, and they collect rewards such as nectar and pollen. Wind and water pollination are passive, and cleistogamous flowers self-pollinate without opening.', '{"a": "Wind-pollinated flowers are usually small and without nectar or scent.", "c": "Water-pollinated flowers do not need to attract animals.", "d": "Cleistogamous flowers never open."}'::jsonb, 'Floral rewards: nectar and pollen; Yucca and its moth are an obligate mutualism.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f3462c1a-02a6-5335-9539-54271f90e9ae', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 'Two blocks A and B of masses 3m and m respectively are connected by a massless and inextensible string. The whole system is suspended by a massless spring as shown in figure. The magnitudes of acceleration of A and B immediately after the string is cut, are respectively:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "g, g/3"}, {"id": "b", "text": "g/3, g"}, {"id": "c", "text": "g, g"}, {"id": "d", "text": "g/3, g/3"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The spring force (4mg) does not change at once, so A gets g/3 upward and B falls freely with g.', 'Before cutting, the spring holds both blocks, so its force is 4mg. Right after cutting, the spring is still stretched: A has net force 4mg − 3mg = mg, so a_A = mg/3m = g/3 (upward). B has only gravity, so a_B = g.', '{"a": "Swaps the two accelerations.", "c": "Ignores the spring force still acting on A.", "d": "B is in free fall, so its acceleration is g."}'::jsonb, 'A spring force cannot change instantly; a string tension can.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/91.webp'),
('5a710f86-a3f1-5ed8-80d4-4cc948e8a3c8', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 'The acceleration due to gravity at a height 1 km above the earth is the same as at a depth d below the surface of earth. Then:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "d = 1/2 km"}, {"id": "b", "text": "d = 1 km"}, {"id": "c", "text": "d = 3/2 km"}, {"id": "d", "text": "d = 2 km"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'For small h, g(1 − 2h/R) = g(1 − d/R), so d = 2h = 2 km.', 'At a small height h, g_h ≈ g(1 − 2h/R). At depth d, g_d = g(1 − d/R). Setting them equal gives d = 2h = 2 × 1 km = 2 km.', '{"a": "Uses the wrong factor (d = h/2).", "b": "Assumes g decreases at the same rate with height and depth.", "c": "Has no basis in the formulas."}'::jsonb, 'g decreases twice as fast with small height as with depth.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f10dfd3f-b1cb-5e1b-b798-b747cb72a8b3', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 'A particle executes linear simple harmonic motion with an amplitude of 3 cm. When the particle is at 2 cm from the mean position, the magnitude of its velocity is equal to that of its acceleration. Then its time period in seconds is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "√5/π"}, {"id": "b", "text": "√5/(2π)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4π/√5"}, {"id": "d", "text": "2π/√3"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Setting ω√(A² − x²) = ω²x gives ω = √5/2, so T = 4π/√5.', 'v = ω√(A² − x²) = ω√(9 − 4) = √5 ω and |a| = ω²x = 2ω². Equating gives ω = √5/2 rad s⁻¹. T = 2π/ω = 4π/√5 s.', '{"a": "This is 1/T, the frequency, not the period.", "b": "Wrong inversion of ω.", "d": "Uses √3 instead of √(A² − x²) = √5."}'::jsonb, 'In SHM, v = ω√(A² − x²) and a = −ω²x.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('e928133c-456c-5624-9b57-29b9b740b519', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Current Electricity', 'The resistance of a wire is ''R'' ohm. If it is melted and stretched to ''n'' times its original length, its new resistance will be:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "nR"}, {"id": "b", "text": "R/n"}, {"id": "c", "text": "n²R"}, {"id": "d", "text": "R/n²"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'At constant volume, R ∝ l², so the new resistance is n²R.', 'Volume is conserved, so A'' = A/n when l'' = nl. R'' = ρ(nl)/(A/n) = n²ρl/A = n²R.', '{"a": "Ignores the reduction in cross-sectional area.", "b": "Resistance increases on stretching.", "d": "Resistance increases on stretching, not decreases."}'::jsonb, 'For a wire stretched at constant volume, R ∝ l² ∝ 1/A².', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f7ef7093-be72-5fcb-82a4-43817b647a21', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance', 'A capacitor is charged by a battery. The battery is removed and another identical uncharged capacitor is connected in parallel. The total electrostatic energy of resulting system:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "increases by a factor of 4"}, {"id": "b", "text": "decreases by a factor of 2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "remains the same"}, {"id": "d", "text": "increases by a factor of 2"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Charge Q is conserved and capacitance doubles, so U = Q²/2C becomes half.', 'Initially U = Q²/2C. After connection the same charge Q sits on 2C, so U'' = Q²/(2 × 2C) = U/2. The lost half is dissipated as heat and radiation during charge redistribution.', '{"a": "Energy cannot increase without an external source.", "c": "Energy is lost during charge sharing.", "d": "Energy cannot increase without an external source."}'::jsonb, 'Joining identical capacitors (one charged, one not) always loses half the energy.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('43a214d5-f0ca-5014-9473-66aa55aef532', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 'Two rods A and B of different materials are welded together as shown in figure. Their thermal conductivities are K₁ and K₂. The thermal conductivity of the composite rod will be:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(K₁ + K₂)/2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3(K₁ + K₂)/2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "K₁ + K₂"}, {"id": "d", "text": "2(K₁ + K₂)"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Two equal-area rods in parallel have an equivalent conductivity (K₁ + K₂)/2.', 'Both rods have length d and the same area A between T₁ and T₂, so they conduct in parallel. Total heat flow: K(2A)ΔT/d = K₁AΔT/d + K₂AΔT/d, giving K = (K₁ + K₂)/2.', '{"b": "Has no basis; it is too large.", "c": "Forgets that the total area is 2A.", "d": "Overestimates; this is not a parallel combination formula."}'::jsonb, 'Parallel conduction: K_eq = (K₁A₁ + K₂A₂)/(A₁ + A₂); series (equal lengths): K_eq = 2K₁K₂/(K₁ + K₂).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/96.webp'),
('1c7304a1-e1fa-598e-a979-f45a1998a3e8', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Waves', 'The two nearest harmonics of a tube closed at one end and open at other end are 220 Hz and 260 Hz. What is the fundamental frequency of the system?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "10 Hz"}, {"id": "b", "text": "20 Hz"}, {"id": "c", "text": "30 Hz"}, {"id": "d", "text": "40 Hz"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'A closed pipe has only odd harmonics, which differ by 2f₀, so 2f₀ = 40 Hz and f₀ = 20 Hz.', 'In a closed pipe, frequencies are (2n − 1)f₀. Consecutive allowed harmonics differ by 2f₀, so 260 − 220 = 40 = 2f₀, which gives f₀ = 20 Hz. Check: 220 = 11 × 20 and 260 = 13 × 20, both odd multiples.', '{"a": "Halves the correct value.", "c": "220 and 260 are not odd multiples of 30.", "d": "Takes the difference as f₀, which is true only for an open pipe."}'::jsonb, 'Closed pipe: only odd harmonics f₀, 3f₀, 5f₀, …', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a537ea38-d2f1-5fd7-90fc-6cad69f39a6c', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 'The bulk modulus of a spherical object is ''B''. If it is subjected to uniform pressure ''p'', the fractional decrease in radius is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "p/B"}, {"id": "b", "text": "B/(3p)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3p/B"}, {"id": "d", "text": "p/(3B)"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'ΔV/V = p/B and ΔV/V = 3ΔR/R, so ΔR/R = p/3B.', 'Bulk modulus B = p/(ΔV/V), so ΔV/V = p/B. For a sphere V ∝ R³, so ΔV/V = 3ΔR/R. Thus ΔR/R = p/(3B).', '{"a": "This is the fractional change in volume, not radius.", "b": "Inverted expression.", "c": "Multiplies by 3 instead of dividing."}'::jsonb, 'For a sphere, fractional volume change = 3 × fractional radius change.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('bc8ec118-3ad9-5ad7-9138-a30ee886dfe1', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 'A physical quantity of the dimensions of length that can be formed out of c, G and e²/(4πε₀) is [c is velocity of light, G is universal constant of gravitation and e is charge]:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(1/c²)[G e²/(4πε₀)]^(1/2)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "c²[G e²/(4πε₀)]^(1/2)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(1/c²)[e²/(G 4πε₀)]^(1/2)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(1/c) G e²/(4πε₀)"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '√(G e²/4πε₀) has dimensions L³T⁻², so dividing by c² gives length.', 'e²/(4πε₀) = F r², with dimensions ML³T⁻². G has dimensions M⁻¹L³T⁻², so G e²/(4πε₀) has L⁶T⁻⁴ and its square root has L³T⁻². Dividing by c² (L²T⁻²) leaves L.', '{"b": "Multiplying by c² gives L⁵T⁻⁴.", "c": "e²/(4πε₀G) has dimensions M², so this gives M L⁻² T², not length.", "d": "Gives L⁵T⁻³."}'::jsonb, 'e²/(4πε₀) has the same dimensions as Gm², i.e. ML³T⁻².', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('735d5cf0-8433-5847-a4b1-b3923403484f', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 'One end of string of length l is connected to a particle of mass ''m'' and the other end is connected to a small peg on a smooth horizontal table. If the particle moves in circle with speed ''v'', the net force on the particle (directed towards center) will be (T represents the tension in the string)', '[{"id": "a", "text": "T"}, {"id": "b", "text": "T + mv²/l"}, {"id": "c", "text": "T − mv²/l"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Zero"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'On a smooth horizontal table, tension is the only horizontal force, so it is the net centripetal force: T.', 'Gravity and the normal reaction cancel vertically. The only horizontal force on the particle is the string tension, which points to the centre. So the net force toward the centre is T, and T = mv²/l.', '{"b": "Counts the centripetal force twice; mv²/l is not an extra force.", "c": "Treats centrifugal force as real in the ground frame.", "d": "A body in circular motion needs a net centripetal force."}'::jsonb, 'Centripetal force is not a new force; it is the net radial force from real interactions.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('de9d563a-991d-5e26-8ba0-6e6195151303', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 'A rope is wound around a hollow cylinder of mass 3 kg and radius 40 cm. What is the angular acceleration of the cylinder if the rope is pulled with a force of 30 N?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "25 m/s²"}, {"id": "b", "text": "0.25 rad/s²"}, {"id": "c", "text": "25 rad/s²"}, {"id": "d", "text": "5 m/s²"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'α = τ/I = (30 × 0.4)/(3 × 0.4²) = 25 rad s⁻².', 'For a hollow cylinder about its axis, I = MR² = 3 × 0.16 = 0.48 kg m². Torque τ = FR = 30 × 0.4 = 12 N m. α = τ/I = 12/0.48 = 25 rad s⁻².', '{"a": "The value is right but the unit is wrong for angular acceleration.", "b": "Calculation error by a factor of 100.", "d": "Wrong value and wrong unit."}'::jsonb, 'Hollow cylinder I = MR²; solid cylinder I = MR²/2 about the axis.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('01a67deb-012b-55fb-a3bb-7c231ae0c8fa', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Waves', 'Two cars moving in opposite directions approach each other with speed of 22 m/s and 16.5 m/s respectively. The driver of the first car blows a horn having a frequency 400 Hz. The frequency heard by the driver of the second car is [velocity of sound 340 m/s]:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "350 Hz"}, {"id": "b", "text": "361 Hz"}, {"id": "c", "text": "411 Hz"}, {"id": "d", "text": "448 Hz"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'f'' = 400 × (340 + 16.5)/(340 − 22) ≈ 448 Hz.', 'Source and observer approach each other, so f'' = f(v + v_o)/(v − v_s) = 400 × 356.5/318 ≈ 448.4 Hz. Both motions raise the apparent frequency.', '{"a": "Frequency must increase when they approach.", "b": "Treats the cars as moving apart.", "c": "Mixes up the signs of the source and observer speeds."}'::jsonb, 'Doppler: f'' = f(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s); use the upper signs for approach.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('1e22e913-39ac-57b7-9edd-7d846c8d3c7c', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Electromagnetic Induction', 'A long solenoid of diameter 0.1 m has 2 × 10⁴ turns per meter. At the centre of the solenoid, a coil of 100 turns and radius 0.01 m is placed with its axis coinciding with the solenoid axis. The current in the solenoid reduces at a constant rate to 0 A from 4 A in 0.05 s. If the resistance of the coil is 10π² Ω, the total charge flowing through the coil during this time is:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "32π μC"}, {"id": "b", "text": "16 μC"}, {"id": "c", "text": "32 μC"}, {"id": "d", "text": "16π μC"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'q = NΔΦ/R = 100 × (μ₀nI × πr²)/(10π²) = 32 μC.', 'B = μ₀nI = 4π×10⁻⁷ × 2×10⁴ × 4 = 32π×10⁻³ T. Flux through the coil: NBπr² = 100 × 32π×10⁻³ × π×10⁻⁴ = 32π²×10⁻⁵ Wb. Charge q = ΔΦ_total/R = 32π²×10⁻⁵/(10π²) = 3.2×10⁻⁵ C = 32 μC.', '{"a": "Leaves an extra factor of π.", "b": "Half the correct value.", "d": "Half the value with an extra π."}'::jsonb, 'Induced charge depends only on change in flux: q = NΔΦ/R, independent of time taken.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('152496e9-a7e0-58c8-a580-316113b96b45', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Electric Charges and Fields', 'Suppose the charge of a proton and an electron differ slightly. One of them is −e, the other is (e + Δe). If the net of electrostatic force and gravitational force between two hydrogen atoms placed at a distance d (much greater than atomic size) apart is zero, then Δe is of the order of [Given mass of hydrogen m_h = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg]', '[{"id": "a", "text": "10⁻²⁰ C"}, {"id": "b", "text": "10⁻²³ C"}, {"id": "c", "text": "10⁻³⁷ C"}, {"id": "d", "text": "10⁻⁴⁷ C"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'kΔe² = Gm², so Δe = m√(G/k) ≈ 1.4 × 10⁻³⁷ C.', 'Each H atom has net charge Δe, so the atoms repel with kΔe²/d² and attract with Gm²/d². Balancing gives Δe = m√(G/k) = 1.67×10⁻²⁷ × √(6.67×10⁻¹¹/9×10⁹) ≈ 1.67×10⁻²⁷ × 8.6×10⁻¹¹ ≈ 1.4×10⁻³⁷ C.', '{"a": "Far too large an imbalance.", "b": "Far too large an imbalance.", "d": "Too small by ten orders of magnitude."}'::jsonb, 'The ratio of electric to gravitational force between two protons is about 10³⁶.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('38f598dd-07ff-5589-9feb-256ae9484e64', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 'Two astronauts are floating in gravitational free space after having lost contact with their spaceship. The two will:', '[{"id": "a", "text": "keep floating at the same distance between them."}, {"id": "b", "text": "move towards each other."}, {"id": "c", "text": "move away from each other."}, {"id": "d", "text": "will become stationary."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Their mutual gravitational attraction pulls them slowly toward each other.', 'With no external field, the only force between the astronauts is their mutual gravitational attraction. This net force on each causes acceleration toward the other, so they gradually move closer.', '{"a": "Ignores their mutual gravitational attraction.", "c": "Gravity is always attractive.", "d": "A net force means they cannot stay at rest."}'::jsonb, 'Gravitation acts between every pair of masses, however small.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('6b6f38cf-bc5c-575e-9917-e945fb9fe3d7', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Atoms', 'The ratio of wavelengths of the last line of Balmer series and the last line of Lyman series is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4"}, {"id": "d", "text": "0.5"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Series limits: Balmer 4/R, Lyman 1/R, so the ratio is 4.', 'The last line is the series limit (n₂ → ∞). For Balmer, 1/λ = R(1/2²) gives λ = 4/R. For Lyman, 1/λ = R(1/1²) gives λ = 1/R. The ratio is (4/R)/(1/R) = 4.', '{"a": "Uses n² ratio incorrectly (2 instead of 2²).", "b": "Would need both series to end at the same level.", "d": "Inverts the ratio (Lyman/Balmer = 1/4, not 0.5)."}'::jsonb, 'Series limit wavelength = n₁²/R; Lyman limit ≈ 91.2 nm, Balmer limit ≈ 364.8 nm.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('217f4f69-0190-52fc-a637-cbc93479ead1', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter', 'The de-Broglie wavelength of a neutron in thermal equilibrium with heavy water at a temperature T (Kelvin) and mass m, is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "h/√(mkT)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "h/√(3mkT)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "2h/√(3mkT)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "2h/√(mkT)"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'λ = h/p with p = √(2m·(3/2)kT) = √(3mkT).', 'A thermal neutron has average kinetic energy (3/2)kT. Then p = √(2mK) = √(3mkT), and λ = h/p = h/√(3mkT).', '{"a": "Uses K = kT/2 instead of (3/2)kT.", "c": "Extra factor of 2 has no basis.", "d": "Wrong energy and an extra factor of 2."}'::jsonb, 'λ = h/√(2mK); for thermal particles K = (3/2)kT.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('8e45c2a9-64f4-55fb-a805-b792b8ceeee2', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Ray Optics and Optical Instruments', 'A thin prism having refracting angle 10° is made of glass of refractive index 1.42. This prism is combined with another thin prism of glass of refractive index 1.7. This combination produces dispersion without deviation. The refracting angle of second prism should be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "4°"}, {"id": "b", "text": "6°"}, {"id": "c", "text": "8°"}, {"id": "d", "text": "10°"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Zero net deviation: (μ₁−1)A₁ = (μ₂−1)A₂ gives A₂ = 6°.', 'For no deviation the two thin-prism deviations must cancel: (1.42−1)(10°) = (1.7−1)A₂. So 4.2 = 0.7A₂, giving A₂ = 6°.', '{"a": "Arithmetic slip; 0.7×4° = 2.8° ≠ 4.2°.", "c": "Gives deviation 5.6°, not 4.2°.", "d": "Equal angles would give unequal deviations because μ differs."}'::jsonb, 'Thin prism deviation δ = (μ−1)A; for no deviation, δ₁ = δ₂ with prisms inverted.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('6e8636b3-ecb2-562c-8df8-38d6ed983c4e', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 'Thermodynamic processes are indicated in the following diagram.
Match the following:
Column-1 | Column-2
P. Process I | a. Adiabatic
Q. Process II | b. Isobaric
R. Process III | c. Isochoric
S. Process IV | d. Isothermal', '[{"id": "a", "text": "P→a, Q→c, R→d, S→b"}, {"id": "b", "text": "P→c, Q→a, R→d, S→b"}, {"id": "c", "text": "P→c, Q→d, R→b, S→a"}, {"id": "d", "text": "P→d, Q→b, R→a, S→c"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'I vertical = isochoric, II steeper than isotherm = adiabatic, III along isotherm = isothermal, IV horizontal = isobaric.', 'Process I is a vertical line (constant V), so it is isochoric. Process IV is horizontal (constant P), so it is isobaric. Process III runs along the 500 K curve and is isothermal. Process II drops from 500 K to 300 K along a curve steeper than the isotherm, which is adiabatic.', '{"a": "Calls the vertical line adiabatic.", "c": "Swaps isothermal, isobaric and adiabatic.", "d": "Mismatches every process."}'::jsonb, 'On a P–V diagram an adiabatic curve is steeper than an isotherm by a factor γ.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/113.webp'),
('2cb6cc33-0d0a-503d-b48f-b70831090cb5', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 'A U tube with both ends open to the atmosphere, is partially filled with water. Oil, which is immiscible with water, is poured into one side until it stands at a distance of 10 mm above the water level on the other side. Meanwhile the water rises by 65 mm from its original level (see diagram). The density of the oil is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "650 kg m⁻³"}, {"id": "b", "text": "425 kg m⁻³"}, {"id": "c", "text": "800 kg m⁻³"}, {"id": "d", "text": "928 kg m⁻³"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Equate pressures at the oil–water interface level: ρ_oil(140 mm) = ρ_w(130 mm).', 'Water falls 65 mm on the oil side and rises 65 mm on the other, so the water column above the interface level B–C is 130 mm. The oil column is 130 + 10 = 140 mm. So ρ_oil × 140 = 1000 × 130, giving ρ_oil ≈ 928 kg m⁻³.', '{"a": "Uses 65/100 ratio incorrectly.", "b": "No consistent column heights give this.", "c": "Would need oil column 162.5 mm."}'::jsonb, 'Pressures at the same horizontal level in a continuous liquid are equal.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/114.webp'),
('415a6109-9217-54b8-b8f4-3ab76bca8126', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 'A spring of force constant k is cut into lengths of ratio 1 : 2 : 3. They are connected in series and the new force constant is k′. Then they are connected in parallel and force constant is k″. Then k′ : k″ is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1 : 6"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1 : 9"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1 : 11"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1 : 14"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Pieces are 6k, 3k, 2k; series gives k, parallel gives 11k.', 'Spring constant is inversely proportional to length, so the pieces have 6k, 3k and 2k. In series they rebuild the original spring, k′ = k. In parallel, k″ = 6k + 3k + 2k = 11k. Ratio 1 : 11.', '{"a": "Uses only the largest piece.", "b": "Arithmetic error in the parallel sum.", "d": "No valid combination gives 14k."}'::jsonb, 'k ∝ 1/length; series: 1/k = Σ1/kᵢ, parallel: k = Σkᵢ.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('0ddac157-1d2d-53ec-946b-ee79f9861b36', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 'Which of the following statements are correct ?
(a) Centre of mass of a body always coincides with the centre of gravity of the body.
(b) Centre of mass of a body is the point at which the total gravitational torque on the body is zero.
(c) A couple on a body produce both translational and rotational motion in a body.
(d) Mechanical advantage greater than one means that small effort can be used to lift a large load.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(b) and (d)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "(a) and (b)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(b) and (c)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(c) and (d)"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '(a) fails in non-uniform g and (c) fails because a couple gives no net force.', 'CM and CG coincide only in a uniform gravitational field, so (a) is not always true. In a uniform field the gravitational torque about the CM is zero, so (b) holds. A couple has zero net force and gives only rotation, so (c) is false. MA > 1 means load > effort, so (d) is true.', '{"b": "(a) is not always true.", "c": "(c) is false: a couple gives only rotation.", "d": "(c) is false."}'::jsonb, 'A couple produces pure rotation; CG = CM only in a uniform g field.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('155e94e4-d5bf-5ae9-bfaf-31959a54ceb5', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Ray Optics and Optical Instruments', 'A beam of light from a source L is incident normally on a plane mirror fixed at a certain distance x from the source. The beam is reflected back as a spot on a scale placed just above the source L. When the mirror is rotated through a small angle θ, the spot of the light is found to move through a distance y on the scale. The angle θ is given by :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "y/2x"}, {"id": "b", "text": "y/x"}, {"id": "c", "text": "x/2y"}, {"id": "d", "text": "x/y"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Rotating a mirror by θ turns the reflected ray by 2θ, so y = 2θx.', 'When the mirror turns by θ the reflected ray turns by 2θ. For small angles the spot shifts by y = x·2θ, so θ = y/(2x).', '{"b": "Forgets that the reflected ray turns by 2θ.", "c": "Inverted expression.", "d": "Inverted and missing the factor 2."}'::jsonb, 'Rotating a plane mirror by θ rotates the reflected ray by 2θ.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a69bb195-5796-5446-95b2-9edf771a33fb', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 'A gas mixture consists of 2 moles of O₂ and 4 moles of Ar at temperature T. Neglecting all vibrational modes, the total internal energy of the system is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "4 RT"}, {"id": "b", "text": "15 RT"}, {"id": "c", "text": "9 RT"}, {"id": "d", "text": "11 RT"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'U = 2(5/2)RT + 4(3/2)RT = 11RT.', 'O₂ is diatomic with 5 degrees of freedom (no vibration), so U = 2 × (5/2)RT = 5RT. Ar is monatomic with 3 degrees of freedom, so U = 4 × (3/2)RT = 6RT. Total = 11RT.', '{"a": "Uses wrong degrees of freedom.", "b": "Treats Ar as diatomic (2×5/2 + 4×5/2 = 15).", "c": "Treats O₂ as monatomic (3 + 6)."}'::jsonb, 'U = (f/2)nRT; f = 3 for monatomic, 5 for diatomic (rigid).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('6f5ce2cc-61f7-5769-8450-936a690118a9', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 'Consider a drop of rain water having mass 1 g falling from a height of 1 km. It hits the ground with a speed of 50 m/s. Take ''g'' constant with a value 10 m/s². The work done by the (i) gravitational force and the (ii) resistive force of air is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(i) −10 J (ii) −8.25 J"}, {"id": "b", "text": "(i) 1.25 J (ii) −8.25 J"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(i) 100 J (ii) 8.75 J"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(i) 10 J (ii) −8.75 J"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'W_g = mgh = 10 J; W_air = ΔK − W_g = 1.25 − 10 = −8.75 J.', 'W_g = 10⁻³ × 10 × 1000 = 10 J. Final KE = ½ × 10⁻³ × 50² = 1.25 J. By the work–energy theorem, W_g + W_air = 1.25 J, so W_air = −8.75 J.', '{"a": "Gravity does positive work on a falling drop.", "b": "1.25 J is the KE, not the work by gravity.", "c": "Wrong mass unit and wrong sign for air resistance."}'::jsonb, 'Work–energy theorem: total work = change in kinetic energy.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('06de4ce6-5404-538b-b37a-7f00ceb2e2cf', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 'A carnot engine having an efficiency of 1/10 as heat engine, is used as a refrigerator. If the work done on the system is 10 J, the amount of energy absorbed from the reservoir at lower temperature is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1 J"}, {"id": "b", "text": "90 J"}, {"id": "c", "text": "99 J"}, {"id": "d", "text": "100 J"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'COP = (1−η)/η = 9, so Q₂ = 9 × 10 = 90 J.', 'For a reversible engine reversed, β = Q₂/W = (1 − η)/η = (9/10)/(1/10) = 9. Hence Q₂ = 9 × 10 J = 90 J.', '{"a": "Uses η × W.", "c": "No basis.", "d": "100 J is the heat rejected to the hot reservoir (Q₁ = Q₂ + W)."}'::jsonb, 'Refrigerator COP β = Q₂/W = T₂/(T₁ − T₂).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('60b1407f-6270-5923-95d6-80095ad917a0', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Moving Charges and Magnetism', 'An arrangement of three parallel straight wires placed perpendicular to plane of paper carrying same current ''I'' along the same direction is shown in Fig. Magnitude of force per unit length on the middle wire ''B'' is given by :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "μ₀i²/(2πd)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2μ₀i²/(πd)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "√2μ₀i²/(πd)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "μ₀i²/(√2πd)"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Two equal perpendicular attractions of μ₀i²/(2πd) give √2 times that.', 'Each of A and C attracts B with force per length μ₀i²/(2πd), along BA and BC, which are perpendicular. The resultant is √2 × μ₀i²/(2πd) = μ₀i²/(√2πd).', '{"a": "Force from only one wire.", "b": "Factor-of-4 error.", "c": "Twice the correct value."}'::jsonb, 'Force per length between parallel wires F/l = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd); like currents attract.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/121.webp'),
('fc8aba1b-2c43-5541-a06c-fbe1db0bf4fb', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 'The x and y coordinates of the particle at any time are x = 5t − 2t² and y = 10t respectively, where x and y are in meters and t in seconds. The acceleration of the particle at t = 2 s is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "0"}, {"id": "b", "text": "5 m/s²"}, {"id": "c", "text": "−4 m/s²"}, {"id": "d", "text": "−8 m/s²"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'aₓ = d²x/dt² = −4, a_y = 0.', 'Differentiating twice: aₓ = −4 m/s² (constant) and a_y = 0. So the acceleration is −4 m/s² at every instant, including t = 2 s.', '{"a": "Only a_y is zero.", "b": "Confuses velocity with acceleration.", "d": "Multiplies by t = 2 wrongly."}'::jsonb, 'Acceleration = second derivative of position; constant for quadratic x(t).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('1e2f6494-c672-5127-82b9-c50fd1b792af', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Ray Optics and Optical Instruments', 'The ratio of resolving powers of an optical microscope for two wavelengths λ₁ = 4000 Å and λ₂ = 6000 Å is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "8 : 27"}, {"id": "b", "text": "9 : 4"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3 : 2"}, {"id": "d", "text": "16 : 81"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'RP ∝ 1/λ, so RP₁ : RP₂ = 6000 : 4000 = 3 : 2.', 'Resolving power of a microscope is 2n sinθ/(1.22λ), inversely proportional to λ. So RP₁/RP₂ = λ₂/λ₁ = 6000/4000 = 3/2.', '{"a": "Uses cube of inverse ratio.", "b": "Uses square of inverse ratio.", "d": "Uses fourth power."}'::jsonb, 'Shorter wavelength gives higher resolving power (basis of electron microscopes).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('984afa96-2110-5c92-a6c2-1dbaf138fe96', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 'Preeti reached the metro station and found that the escalator was not working. She walked up the stationary escalator in time t₁. On other days, if she remains stationary on the moving escalator, then the escalator takes her up in time t₂. The time taken by her to walk up on the moving escalator will be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(t₁ + t₂)/2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "t₁t₂/(t₂ − t₁)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "t₁t₂/(t₂ + t₁)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "t₁ − t₂"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Speeds add: L/t = L/t₁ + L/t₂.', 'Her speed is L/t₁ and the escalator''s is L/t₂. Walking on the moving escalator, speed = L/t₁ + L/t₂, so t = t₁t₂/(t₁ + t₂).', '{"a": "Arithmetic mean has no physical basis here.", "b": "Corresponds to walking against the escalator.", "d": "Could be negative; dimensionally fine but wrong."}'::jsonb, 'Relative velocity: velocities add when motions are in the same direction.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d33c7d21-45b2-5d6b-ad0e-9eca19b519f7', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 'A spherical black body with a radius of 12 cm radiates 450 watt power at 500 K. If the radius were halved and the temperature doubled, the power radiated in watt would be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "225"}, {"id": "b", "text": "450"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1000"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1800"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'P ∝ r²T⁴: (1/4)(16) = 4 times, so 1800 W.', 'By Stefan''s law, P = σ(4πr²)T⁴. Halving r multiplies P by 1/4, and doubling T multiplies it by 16. Net factor 4, so P = 4 × 450 = 1800 W.', '{"a": "Ignores the T⁴ dependence.", "b": "Assumes the changes cancel.", "c": "No valid scaling gives this."}'::jsonb, 'Stefan–Boltzmann law: P = σAT⁴ for a black body.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('b2ad4e0e-eb66-5583-b360-0f1402158b57', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Semiconductor Electronics: Materials, Devices and Simple Circuits', 'The given electrical network is equivalent to :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "AND gate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "OR gate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "NOR gate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "NOT gate"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'NOR followed by two inverters is still NOR.', 'The first gate gives (A + B)′. The second NOR has both inputs shorted, so it acts as NOT and gives A + B. The final NOT gives (A + B)′, which is a NOR gate.', '{"a": "AND would need inverted inputs into a NOR.", "b": "OR is the output of the middle stage, not the final output.", "d": "The circuit has two inputs, not one."}'::jsonb, 'A NOR or NAND gate with its inputs tied together works as a NOT gate.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/127.webp'),
('bf6922cc-d52f-5cf0-bd93-6e2846f628a8', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Semiconductor Electronics: Materials, Devices and Simple Circuits', 'In a common emitter transistor amplifier the audio signal voltage across the collector is 3 V. The resistance of collector is 3 kΩ. If current gain is 100 and the base resistance is 2 kΩ, the voltage and power gain of the amplifier is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "200 and 1000"}, {"id": "b", "text": "15 and 200"}, {"id": "c", "text": "150 and 15000"}, {"id": "d", "text": "20 and 2000"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'A_v = β(R_C/R_B) = 150; A_p = β·A_v = 15000.', 'Voltage gain A_v = β × R_C/R_B = 100 × 3/2 = 150. Power gain = current gain × voltage gain = 100 × 150 = 15000.', '{"a": "Inverts the resistance ratio.", "b": "Wrong use of the resistance ratio.", "d": "No consistent calculation gives this."}'::jsonb, 'CE amplifier: A_v = β R_C/R_B and A_p = β·A_v.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('80612751-bd2b-5e4d-bec2-9eb514afce6e', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 'Two discs of same moment of inertia rotating about their regular axis passing through centre and perpendicular to the plane of disc with angular velocities ω₁ and ω₂. They are brought into contact face to face coinciding the axis of rotation. The expression for loss of energy during this process is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "½ I(ω₁ + ω₂)²"}, {"id": "b", "text": "¼ I(ω₁ − ω₂)²"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I(ω₁ − ω₂)²"}, {"id": "d", "text": "⅛ (ω₁ − ω₂)²"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Common ω = (ω₁+ω₂)/2; KE loss = I(ω₁−ω₂)²/4.', 'Angular momentum is conserved: Iω₁ + Iω₂ = 2Iω, so ω = (ω₁ + ω₂)/2. Initial KE = ½I(ω₁² + ω₂²) and final KE = I(ω₁ + ω₂)²/4. Loss = (I/4)(ω₁ − ω₂)².', '{"a": "Not a difference term; would be non-zero for equal ω.", "c": "Four times too large.", "d": "Wrong coefficient and missing I."}'::jsonb, 'Angular momentum is conserved when no external torque acts, but KE is lost in such ''rotational collisions''.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d3e89846-ccd3-552f-b943-532e2beecc80', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Wave Optics', 'Young''s double slit experiment is first performed in air and then in a medium other than air. It is found that 8th bright fringe in the medium lies where 5th dark fringe lies in air. The refractive index of the medium is nearly :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1.25"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1.59"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1.69"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1.78"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '8λD/(μd) = 4.5λD/d gives μ = 8/4.5 ≈ 1.78.', 'The 8th bright fringe in the medium is at y = 8λD/(μd), since the wavelength becomes λ/μ. The 5th dark fringe in air is at y = (2×5 − 1)λD/(2d) = 4.5λD/d. Equating gives μ = 8/4.5 ≈ 1.78.', '{"a": "Uses 5/4.", "b": "No consistent fringe counting gives this.", "c": "No consistent fringe counting gives this."}'::jsonb, 'In a medium of index μ, wavelength and fringe width both shrink by a factor μ.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a577e1d4-fabf-59e5-8848-ecadd1036f71', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Semiconductor Electronics: Materials, Devices and Simple Circuits', 'Which one of the following represents forward bias diode ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Option (1) in figure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Option (2) in figure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Option (3) in figure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Option (4) in figure"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Forward bias needs the p-side (anode) at higher potential than the n-side.', 'In each circuit the arrow (anode) is on the left. Only in (1) is the anode (0 V) at a higher potential than the cathode side (−2 V), so the diode is forward biased. In (2), (3) and (4) the left end is at a lower potential than the right.', '{"b": "−4 V < −3 V, so reverse biased.", "c": "−2 V < +2 V, so reverse biased.", "d": "3 V < 5 V, so reverse biased."}'::jsonb, 'Forward bias: p-side connected to higher potential than n-side.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/131.webp'),
('308d08de-b49a-5a93-9975-893ace6b1230', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Wave Optics', 'Two Polaroids P₁ and P₂ are placed with their axis perpendicular to each other. Unpolarised light I₀ is incident on P₁. A third polaroid P₃ is kept in between P₁ and P₂ such that its axis makes an angle 45° with that of P₁. The intensity of transmitted light through P₂ is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "I₀/2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "I₀/4"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I₀/8"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I₀/16"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'I₀/2 × cos²45° × cos²45° = I₀/8.', 'P₁ passes I₀/2. P₃ at 45° passes (I₀/2)cos²45° = I₀/4. P₂ is at 45° to P₃, so it passes (I₀/4)cos²45° = I₀/8.', '{"a": "Only the first polaroid''s effect.", "b": "Misses one Malus factor.", "d": "Applies an extra halving."}'::jsonb, 'Malus''s law: I = I₀cos²θ; unpolarised light is halved by the first polaroid.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('a00f87fc-b7b6-589a-b6cf-9357713799a4', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Electromagnetic Waves', 'In an electromagnetic wave in free space the root mean square value of the electric field is E_rms = 6 V/m. The peak value of the magnetic field is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1.41 × 10⁻⁸ T"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2.83 × 10⁻⁸ T"}, {"id": "c", "text": "0.70 × 10⁻⁸ T"}, {"id": "d", "text": "4.23 × 10⁻⁸ T"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'B₀ = √2·E_rms/c = 8.49/(3×10⁸) ≈ 2.83 × 10⁻⁸ T.', 'Peak electric field E₀ = √2 × 6 ≈ 8.49 V/m. Then B₀ = E₀/c = 8.49/(3 × 10⁸) ≈ 2.83 × 10⁻⁸ T.', '{"a": "Divides B_rms (2 × 10⁻⁸ T) by √2 instead of multiplying by √2.", "c": "Half of option (1); has no physical basis.", "d": "Three times option (1); has no physical basis."}'::jsonb, 'In an EM wave E₀ = cB₀ and peak = √2 × rms.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('0cdd8db0-b1e7-5cd8-af24-3982ac92aeee', 'neet_ug', 'Physics', 'Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance', 'The diagrams below show regions of equipotentials.
A positive charge is moved from A to B in each diagram.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Maximum work is required to move q in figure (c)."}, {"id": "b", "text": "In all the four cases the work done is the same."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Minimum work is required to move q in figure (a)."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maximum work is required to move q in figure (b)."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'In every diagram A is at 10 V and B at 40 V, so W = q(V_B − V_A) is the same.', 'Work done by an external agent in moving a charge slowly is W = q(V_B − V_A), which depends only on the end potentials. In all four diagrams A lies on the 10 V surface and B on the 40 V surface, so W = 30q in each case.', '{"a": "Path and spacing do not affect work between equipotentials.", "c": "All cases are equal; none is minimum.", "d": "Curved equipotentials do not change the potential difference."}'::jsonb, 'Electrostatic force is conservative; work depends only on initial and final potentials.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/135.webp'),
('09e2298d-a103-589c-a59b-9a0fa521189a', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 'The reason for greater range of oxidation states in actinoids is attributed to :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "the radioactive nature of actinoids"}, {"id": "b", "text": "actinoid contraction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "5f, 6d and 7s levels having comparable energies"}, {"id": "d", "text": "4f and 5d levels being close in energies"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Actinoids show many oxidation states because 5f, 6d and 7s energies are close.', 'In actinoids, the 5f, 6d and 7s subshells lie close in energy, so electrons from all of them can take part in bonding. This gives a wider range of oxidation states than lanthanoids.', '{"a": "Radioactivity does not decide oxidation states.", "b": "Contraction affects size, not range of oxidation states.", "d": "4f/5d refers to lanthanoids."}'::jsonb, 'Actinoids show oxidation states up to +7 (Np, Pu); lanthanoids mainly +3.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('e8b9d835-ec2f-5d45-a439-9eaf8b7cc73b', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 'An example of a sigma bonded organometallic compound is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Ruthenocene"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Grignard''s reagent"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ferrocene"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cobaltocene"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'RMgX has a direct M–C σ bond.', 'Grignard reagents (RMgX) contain a σ bond between Mg and carbon. Ferrocene, ruthenocene and cobaltocene are sandwich compounds where the metal is π-bonded to cyclopentadienyl rings.', '{"a": "Sandwich π-complex.", "c": "Sandwich π-complex.", "d": "Sandwich π-complex."}'::jsonb, 'Grignard reagent and (C₂H₅)₄Pb are σ-bonded organometallics; ferrocene is π-bonded.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d10ad221-1eb3-51f2-8590-ef281fcff24b', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Structure of Atom', 'Which one is the wrong statement ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "de-Broglie''s wavelength is given by λ = h/(mv), where m = mass of the particle, v = group velocity of the particle."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The uncertainty principle is ΔE × Δt ≥ h/4π."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Half filled and fully filled orbitals have greater stability due to greater exchange energy, greater symmetry and more balanced arrangement."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The energy of 2s orbital is less than the energy of 2p orbital in case of Hydrogen like atoms."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'In one-electron species, orbital energy depends only on n, so 2s and 2p are degenerate.', 'For hydrogen and hydrogen-like ions, energy depends only on the principal quantum number, so 2s and 2p have equal energy. Statement (4) is therefore wrong. The other statements are correct forms of de Broglie''s relation, the energy–time uncertainty relation and the stability of half/fully filled subshells.', '{"a": "Correct; particle velocity equals group velocity of the matter wave.", "b": "Correct energy–time form of the uncertainty principle.", "c": "Correct reason for extra stability of d⁵, d¹⁰ etc."}'::jsonb, 'In H-like atoms orbital energies depend only on n; in multi-electron atoms on n + l.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ffa29f02-89f0-533f-9829-ff4b44820ff5', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties', 'The element Z = 114 has been discovered recently. It will belong to which of the following family/group and electronic configuration ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Halogen family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p⁵"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Carbon family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p²"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oxygen family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p⁴"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nitrogen family, [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p⁶"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Z = 114 = 86 + 28 gives 7s²7p², group 14.', 'After Rn (86), 28 electrons remain: 5f¹⁴ 6d¹⁰ 7s² 7p². Four valence electrons (ns²np²) place the element in group 14, the carbon family.', '{"a": "7p⁵ would be Z = 117.", "c": "7p⁴ would be Z = 116.", "d": "7p⁶ would be Z = 118 and is a noble-gas configuration."}'::jsonb, 'Group number for p-block = 10 + number of valence (ns + np) electrons.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('5faf88e8-fefc-5fa0-9bab-eebf7a7041f3', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 'A 20 litre container at 400 K contains CO₂(g) at pressure 0.4 atm and an excess of SrO (neglect the volume of solid SrO). The volume of the container is now decreased by moving the movable piston fitted in the container. The maximum volume of the container, when pressure of CO₂ attains its maximum value, will be :
(Given that : SrCO₃(s) ⇌ SrO(s) + CO₂(g), Kp = 1.6 atm)', '[{"id": "a", "text": "5 litre"}, {"id": "b", "text": "10 litre"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4 litre"}, {"id": "d", "text": "2 litre"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'CO₂ pressure can rise only to Kp = 1.6 atm; Boyle''s law gives V = 0.4×20/1.6 = 5 L.', 'The maximum CO₂ pressure in equilibrium with SrO/SrCO₃ is Kp = 1.6 atm. Until that pressure is reached no CO₂ is consumed, so P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ gives V = (0.4 × 20)/1.6 = 5 L. Compressing further only converts CO₂ to SrCO₃.', '{"b": "Corresponds to 0.8 atm.", "c": "Corresponds to 2 atm, above Kp.", "d": "Corresponds to 4 atm, above Kp."}'::jsonb, 'For heterogeneous equilibria with only one gas, Kp equals that gas''s equilibrium partial pressure.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('55f72ceb-a36b-5ef5-a3ee-d7fa92459dc8', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 'Predict the correct intermediate and product in the following reaction :
H₃C–C≡CH —(H₂O, H₂SO₄ / HgSO₄)→ intermediate (A) → product (B)', '[{"id": "a", "text": "A: H₃C–C(SO₄)=CH₂ ; B: H₃C–CO–CH₃"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A: H₃C–C(OH)=CH₂ ; B: H₃C–C(SO₄)=CH₂"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A: H₃C–CO–CH₃ ; B: H₃C–C≡CH"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A: H₃C–C(OH)=CH₂ ; B: H₃C–CO–CH₃"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Markovnikov hydration gives the enol prop-1-en-2-ol, which tautomerises to acetone.', 'Hg²⁺-catalysed hydration of propyne adds OH to the more substituted carbon (Markovnikov), giving the enol H₃C–C(OH)=CH₂. This enol tautomerises to the more stable keto form, propanone (CH₃COCH₃).', '{"a": "No sulphate-substituted intermediate forms.", "b": "The enol does not convert into a sulphate.", "c": "Acetone does not revert to propyne."}'::jsonb, 'Hydration of alkynes (other than ethyne) gives ketones via enol tautomerism.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f726d9ba-ddaa-57e6-b175-b3a2418a0975', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 'Which of the following reactions is appropriate for converting acetamide to methanamine ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Carbylamine reaction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hoffmann hypobromamide reaction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stephens reaction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gabriels phthalimide synthesis"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Hofmann bromamide degradation removes one carbon: CH₃CONH₂ → CH₃NH₂.', 'Treating acetamide with Br₂ and aqueous NaOH (Hofmann bromamide degradation) gives a primary amine with one carbon fewer. So CH₃CONH₂ gives CH₃NH₂ (methanamine).', '{"a": "Carbylamine is a test for primary amines giving isocyanides.", "c": "Stephen reaction reduces nitriles to aldehydes.", "d": "Gabriel synthesis starts from alkyl halides, not amides."}'::jsonb, 'Hofmann bromamide: RCONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → RNH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('28e3f76b-6aa8-507c-8fb8-08e996371c44', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 'The species, having bond angles of 120° is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "PH₃"}, {"id": "b", "text": "ClF₃"}, {"id": "c", "text": "NCl₃"}, {"id": "d", "text": "BCl₃"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'BCl₃ is trigonal planar (sp²), with 120° bond angles.', 'Boron in BCl₃ has three bond pairs and no lone pair, so it is sp² hybridised and trigonal planar with 120° angles. PH₃ and NCl₃ are pyramidal and ClF₃ is T-shaped.', '{"a": "Pyramidal, angle about 93°.", "b": "T-shaped, angles near 87.5°.", "c": "Pyramidal, angle about 107°."}'::jsonb, 'AB₃ with no lone pair: trigonal planar, 120°.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ce3d877a-a7c3-50a0-b8b4-579a967165a9', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 'The correct order of the stoichiometries of AgCl formed when AgNO₃ in excess is treated with the complexes : CoCl₃·6NH₃, CoCl₃·5NH₃, CoCl₃·4NH₃ respectively is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1 AgCl, 3 AgCl, 2 AgCl"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3 AgCl, 1 AgCl, 2 AgCl"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3 AgCl, 2 AgCl, 1 AgCl"}, {"id": "d", "text": "2 AgCl, 3 AgCl, 1 AgCl"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Ionisable Cl⁻: [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ (3), [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ (2), [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl (1).', 'Cobalt(III) has coordination number 6. With 6 NH₃ all three Cl⁻ are outside, with 5 NH₃ one Cl is in the sphere and two are ionisable, and with 4 NH₃ only one is ionisable. So AgCl formed is 3, 2 and 1 mol.', '{"a": "Wrong order.", "b": "Wrong order.", "d": "Wrong order."}'::jsonb, 'Only counter-ion chloride precipitates as AgCl (Werner''s experiment).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('93be14d6-b299-56df-a612-a1600b50fb1e', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Thermodynamics', 'For a given reaction, ΔH = 35.5 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = 83.6 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. The reaction is spontaneous at : (Assume that ΔH and ΔS do not vary with temperature)', '[{"id": "a", "text": "T < 425 K"}, {"id": "b", "text": "T > 425 K"}, {"id": "c", "text": "all temperatures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "T > 298 K"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'ΔG < 0 when T > ΔH/ΔS = 35500/83.6 ≈ 425 K.', 'Both ΔH and ΔS are positive, so ΔG = ΔH − TΔS becomes negative only at high temperature. T > 35500/83.6 ≈ 424.6 K, i.e. above about 425 K.', '{"a": "At low T the positive ΔH dominates, so ΔG > 0.", "c": "Needs ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0.", "d": "Between 298 and 425 K ΔG is still positive."}'::jsonb, 'ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0 means spontaneous only above T = ΔH/ΔS.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('eb492833-e7f7-5bca-a279-56276cf1c66a', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'The p-Block Elements', 'Match the interhalogen compounds of column I with the geometry in column II and assign the correct code.
Column I | Column II
(a) XX′ | (i) T-shape
(b) XX′₃ | (ii) Pentagonal bipyramidal
(c) XX′₅ | (iii) Linear
(d) XX′₇ | (iv) Square-pyramidal
(v) Tetrahedral', '[{"id": "a", "text": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(a)-(v), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'XX′ linear, XX′₃ T-shaped, XX′₅ square pyramidal, XX′₇ pentagonal bipyramidal.', 'XX′ is diatomic and linear. XX′₃ (e.g. ClF₃) has 3 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs, giving a T-shape. XX′₅ (e.g. BrF₅) has 5 bp + 1 lp, square pyramidal. XX′₇ (IF₇) has 7 bp, pentagonal bipyramidal.', '{"a": "XX′₃ is not square pyramidal.", "c": "XX′ is linear, not tetrahedral.", "d": "Mismatches every entry."}'::jsonb, 'Interhalogen shapes follow VSEPR: ClF₃ T-shape, BrF₅ square pyramidal, IF₇ pentagonal bipyramidal.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('5ae47f48-bb78-577e-a6bd-84781804203c', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 'Which one of the following statements is not correct?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Catalyst does not initiate any reaction."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The value of equilibrium constant is changed in the presence of a catalyst in the reaction at equilibrium."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Enzymes catalyse mainly bio-chemical reactions."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Coenzymes increase the catalytic activity of enzyme."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'A catalyst speeds up forward and reverse reactions equally, so K is unchanged.', 'A catalyst lowers the activation energy of forward and backward reactions by the same amount. It helps reach equilibrium faster but does not change ΔG° or the equilibrium constant, so statement (2) is wrong.', '{"a": "True; a catalyst only speeds up a reaction that is already feasible.", "c": "True; enzymes are biological catalysts.", "d": "True; coenzymes enhance enzyme activity."}'::jsonb, 'A catalyst does not alter ΔG or K; it only provides a lower-Ea pathway.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('63ddab87-d8cf-52bb-8309-9beb357f2ad7', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'The p-Block Elements', 'Name the gas that can readily decolourise acidified KMnO₄ solution :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "CO₂"}, {"id": "b", "text": "SO₂"}, {"id": "c", "text": "NO₂"}, {"id": "d", "text": "P₂O₅"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'SO₂ is a reducing agent that reduces MnO₄⁻ to Mn²⁺.', 'SO₂ reduces acidified permanganate: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2Mn²⁺ + 5SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺, so the purple colour disappears. CO₂ and P₂O₅ are not reducing, and NO₂ does not readily do this.', '{"a": "CO₂ has carbon in its highest state and is not a reductant.", "c": "NO₂ does not readily decolourise KMnO₄.", "d": "P₂O₅ is a solid acidic oxide, not a reducing gas."}'::jsonb, 'SO₂ decolourises acidified KMnO₄ and turns acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ green.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d826f8f2-b40c-5b5a-a6e7-cacd7fdb5c1f', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 'The correct increasing order of basic strength for the following compounds is :
(I) aniline, (II) p-nitroaniline, (III) p-toluidine', '[{"id": "a", "text": "II < III < I"}, {"id": "b", "text": "III < I < II"}, {"id": "c", "text": "III < II < I"}, {"id": "d", "text": "II < I < III"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '−NO₂ withdraws electrons (weaker base), −CH₃ donates (stronger base).', 'The p-nitro group withdraws electron density by −R and −I effects, making the N lone pair less available, so II is the weakest base. The p-methyl group donates electrons (+I and hyperconjugation), making III the strongest. Order: II < I < III.', '{"a": "Places aniline above p-toluidine.", "b": "Reverses the order.", "c": "Puts p-nitroaniline above p-toluidine."}'::jsonb, 'Electron-donating groups increase and electron-withdrawing groups decrease basicity of aniline.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/152.webp'),
('dbf2640b-3274-5fe1-a64f-1b714cb3db36', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 'If molality of the dilute solution is doubled, the value of molal depression constant (K_f) will be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "doubled"}, {"id": "b", "text": "halved"}, {"id": "c", "text": "tripled"}, {"id": "d", "text": "unchanged"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'K_f depends only on the solvent.', 'K_f = RM T_f²/(1000 ΔH_fus) depends only on properties of the solvent. Changing molality changes ΔT_f, not K_f.', '{"a": "ΔT_f doubles, not K_f.", "b": "No basis.", "c": "No basis."}'::jsonb, 'K_f and K_b are characteristic constants of the solvent.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('03cae57d-1f89-5bd8-9666-0424d1e508cf', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 'Of the following, which is the product formed when cyclohexanone undergoes aldol condensation followed by heating ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Option (1) in figure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Option (2) in figure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Option (3) in figure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Option (4) in figure"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Self-aldol then dehydration gives 2-cyclohexylidenecyclohexan-1-one.', 'The enolate of one cyclohexanone attacks the carbonyl of another, giving a β-hydroxy ketone (aldol). On heating this dehydrates to an α,β-unsaturated ketone: 2-cyclohexylidenecyclohexanone, with one C=O and an exocyclic C=C. This is option (2).', '{"a": "This is the aldol before heating (dehydration not done).", "c": "Has lost the carbonyl group; not an aldol product.", "d": "Has two carbonyls; only one C=O survives aldol condensation."}'::jsonb, 'Aldol condensation = aldol addition + dehydration to an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/154.webp'),
('797722c7-7093-592a-a8cb-0ccc6948dcf2', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 'The equilibrium constants of the following are :
N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃   K₁
N₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2NO   K₂
H₂ + ½O₂ → H₂O   K₃
The equilibrium constant (K) of the reaction :
2NH₃ + 5/2 O₂ ⇌ 2NO + 3H₂O, will be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "K₁K₃³/K₂"}, {"id": "b", "text": "K₂K₃³/K₁"}, {"id": "c", "text": "K₂K₃/K₁"}, {"id": "d", "text": "K₂³K₃/K₁"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Reverse reaction 1, add reaction 2 and three times reaction 3: K = K₂K₃³/K₁.', 'Target = (reverse of eq. 1) + (eq. 2) + 3 × (eq. 3). Reversing divides by K₁, adding multiplies, and scaling by 3 cubes the constant. Hence K = K₂·K₃³/K₁.', '{"a": "Inverts the roles of K₁ and K₂.", "c": "Forgets that 3 mol H₂O need K₃ cubed.", "d": "Cubes K₂ instead of K₃."}'::jsonb, 'Multiplying an equation by n raises K to the nth power; reversing it gives 1/K.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('61d7f56b-a17e-5b50-b3e2-7fac6e723797', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry – Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 'The correct statement regarding electrophile is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Electrophile is a negatively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Electrophile is a negatively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from another electrophile"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Electrophiles are generally neutral species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Electrophile can be either neutral or positively charged species and can form a bond by accepting a pair of electrons from a nucleophile"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Electrophiles are electron-deficient, neutral or cationic, and accept an electron pair from a nucleophile.', 'An electrophile (''electron-loving'') is electron-deficient: either a cation such as NO₂⁺ or a neutral species such as BF₃ or a carbonyl carbon. It forms a bond by accepting an electron pair donated by a nucleophile.', '{"a": "Electrophiles are not negatively charged.", "b": "Wrong charge, and the pair comes from a nucleophile, not another electrophile.", "c": "Cationic electrophiles are very common; they are not ''generally neutral''."}'::jsonb, 'Electrophiles are Lewis acids; nucleophiles are Lewis bases.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('9490c1af-147f-558f-be82-328b95f7c3cd', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Thermodynamics', 'A gas is allowed to expand in a well insulated container against a constant external pressure of 2.5 atm from an initial volume of 2.50 L to a final volume of 4.50 L. The change in internal energy ΔU of the gas in joules will be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1136.25 J"}, {"id": "b", "text": "−500 J"}, {"id": "c", "text": "−505 J"}, {"id": "d", "text": "+505 J"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Adiabatic, so ΔU = w = −PΔV = −5 L atm ≈ −505 J.', 'The container is insulated, so q = 0 and ΔU = w. w = −P_ext ΔV = −2.5 atm × (4.50 − 2.50) L = −5.0 L atm. With 1 L atm ≈ 101 J, ΔU ≈ −505 J.', '{"a": "Not obtainable from the data with correct sign or conversion.", "b": "Uses 100 J per L atm.", "d": "Wrong sign: the gas does work, so its internal energy falls."}'::jsonb, 'For an adiabatic process q = 0, so ΔU = w; 1 L atm = 101.3 J.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('4299579f-b486-5a6e-a809-8c417a2739a0', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 'Which of the following pairs of compounds is isoelectronic and isostructural ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "BeCl₂, XeF₂"}, {"id": "b", "text": "TeI₂, XeF₂"}, {"id": "c", "text": "IBr₂⁻, XeF₂"}, {"id": "d", "text": "IF₃, XeF₂"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'IBr₂⁻ and XeF₂ both have 22 valence electrons and are linear (sp³d, 3 lone pairs).', 'XeF₂: 8 + 2×7 = 22 valence electrons, 3 lone pairs on Xe, linear. IBr₂⁻: 7 + 2×7 + 1 = 22 valence electrons, 3 lone pairs on I, also linear. The two are isoelectronic (in valence electrons) and isostructural.', '{"a": "BeCl₂ is linear but has only 16 valence electrons and no lone pairs on Be.", "b": "TeI₂ has 20 valence electrons and is bent.", "d": "IF₃ is T-shaped with 28 valence electrons."}'::jsonb, 'AX₂E₃ species (XeF₂, I₃⁻, ICl₂⁻) are linear.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('17b76e84-555f-534c-be80-98fef0a78195', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 'The heating of phenyl-methyl ethers with HI produces.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "ethyl chlorides"}, {"id": "b", "text": "iodobenzene"}, {"id": "c", "text": "phenol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "benzene"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'C₆H₅–O–CH₃ + HI → C₆H₅OH + CH₃I.', 'In alkyl aryl ethers the O–aryl bond has partial double-bond character and the phenyl carbon resists SN2 attack. I⁻ therefore attacks the methyl carbon of the protonated ether, giving methyl iodide and phenol.', '{"a": "No ethyl group or chloride is involved.", "b": "The aryl–O bond is not cleaved, so iodobenzene does not form.", "d": "No reduction to benzene occurs."}'::jsonb, 'Anisole + HI → phenol + CH₃I.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d537e7d7-3e33-5d25-932b-5972b1fe5f38', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 'Correct increasing order for the wavelengths of absorption in the visible region for the complexes of Co³⁺ is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "[Co(en)₃]³⁺, [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺"}, {"id": "b", "text": "[Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, [Co(en)₃]³⁺, [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺"}, {"id": "c", "text": "[Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, [Co(en)₃]³⁺"}, {"id": "d", "text": "[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, [Co(en)₃]³⁺, [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Ligand strength en > NH₃ > H₂O, so Δ₀ decreases and λ increases in that order.', 'By the spectrochemical series en > NH₃ > H₂O, so Δ₀ is largest for the en complex. Since Δ₀ = hc/λ, the largest splitting absorbs the shortest wavelength. The increasing order of λ is therefore [Co(en)₃]³⁺ < [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ < [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺.', '{"b": "Places the weakest-field aqua complex first.", "c": "This is the decreasing order of wavelength.", "d": "Puts NH₃ above en in field strength."}'::jsonb, 'Stronger field ligand → larger Δ₀ → shorter absorbed wavelength.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f328a720-61bb-52d3-aaa9-020217fda1e0', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 'Pick out the correct statement with respect to [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "It is sp³d² hybridised and octahedral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It is sp³d² hybridised and tetrahedral"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is d²sp³ hybridised and octahedral"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is dsp² hybridised and square planar"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Mn³⁺ (d⁴) with strong-field CN⁻ pairs up in t₂g, leaving two inner d orbitals: d²sp³, octahedral.', 'Mn is +3 here, so it has a 3d⁴ configuration. Strong-field CN⁻ forces pairing into three 3d orbitals (2 unpaired electrons), freeing two inner 3d orbitals. Six CN⁻ ligands then occupy d²sp³ hybrids, giving an inner-orbital octahedral complex.', '{"a": "sp³d² (outer orbital) is for weak-field ligands.", "b": "sp³d² can never be tetrahedral.", "d": "Six ligands cannot be square planar dsp²."}'::jsonb, 'CN⁻ is a strong-field ligand that favours low-spin inner-orbital (d²sp³) complexes.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('896ee203-ded9-5755-992e-daf926a73fca', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 'With respect to the conformers of ethane, which of the following statements is true ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Bond angle remains same but bond length changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bond angle changes but bond length remains same"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Both bond angle and bond length change"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both bond angles and bond length remains same"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Conformers differ only by rotation about the C–C bond; bond angles and lengths do not change.', 'Staggered, eclipsed and skew forms of ethane arise only from rotation about the C–C σ bond. Only the dihedral angle and the torsional strain change. Bond lengths and bond angles stay the same.', '{"a": "Bond length does not change on rotation.", "b": "Bond angle does not change on rotation.", "c": "Neither parameter changes."}'::jsonb, 'Conformations differ only in dihedral angle; the staggered form is the most stable.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('52473ffd-9e91-51ab-8e2e-3cb46ba19e3b', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry', 'Which of the following is dependent on temperature ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Molality"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Molarity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Mole fraction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Weight percentage"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Molarity uses solution volume, which changes with temperature.', 'Molarity = moles of solute per litre of solution, and volume expands or contracts with temperature. Molality, mole fraction and mass percent depend only on masses or moles, so they do not depend on temperature.', '{"a": "Molality is moles per kg of solvent, which is mass-based.", "c": "Mole fraction is a ratio of moles.", "d": "Mass percentage is a ratio of masses."}'::jsonb, 'Concentration terms that involve volume (M, N, % w/v) are temperature-dependent.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('942a31c5-34a6-5984-b5ed-a29110586020', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following statements is not correct ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Insulin maintains sugar level in the blood of a human body."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ovalbumin is a simple food reserve in egg-white."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Blood proteins thrombin and fibrinogen are involved in blood clotting."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Denaturation makes the proteins more active."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Denaturation destroys a protein''s 2°/3° structure, so it loses its biological activity.', 'Denaturation by heat, pH change and similar agents disrupts the hydrogen bonds and other interactions that hold the secondary and tertiary structure. The protein unfolds and loses its biological activity, as when egg white coagulates. It does not become more active.', '{"a": "True: insulin lowers blood glucose.", "b": "True: ovalbumin is the storage protein of egg white.", "c": "True: thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin in clotting."}'::jsonb, 'Denaturation leaves the primary structure intact but abolishes biological activity.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('4724ca69-5509-5f30-8ccc-737df3cfae4f', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry – Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 'The IUPAC name of the compound [structure shown in figure] is ______.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "3-keto-2-methylhex-4-enal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "5-formylhex-2-en-3-one"}, {"id": "c", "text": "5-methyl-4-oxohex-2-en-5-al"}, {"id": "d", "text": "3-keto-2-methylhex-5-enal"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'OHC–CH(CH₃)–CO–CH=CH–CH₃ is 2-methyl-3-oxohex-4-enal (keto = oxo).', 'The principal group is –CHO, so it is C-1. The six-carbon chain carries a methyl at C-2, a ketone at C-3 and a C=C between C-4 and C-5. The name is 2-methyl-3-oxohex-4-enal, which option (1) writes using ''keto'' for the oxo prefix.', '{"b": "The aldehyde has priority and must be the suffix, not ''formyl''.", "c": "Numbering from the wrong end; the -al carbon must be C-1.", "d": "The double bond is at C-4, not C-5."}'::jsonb, 'Aldehyde outranks ketone; the CHO carbon is C-1 of the chain.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/166.webp'),
('33a26127-0085-5036-b90e-b3a960b8d816', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'The p-Block Elements', 'HgCl₂ and I₂ both when dissolved in water containing I⁻ ions the pair of species formed is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "HgI₂, I₃⁻"}, {"id": "b", "text": "HgI₂, I⁻"}, {"id": "c", "text": "HgI₄²⁻, I₃⁻"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hg₂I₂, I⁻"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Excess I⁻ converts HgCl₂ to soluble K₂[HgI₄] and I₂ to I₃⁻.', 'HgCl₂ + 2I⁻ gives HgI₂, which dissolves in more I⁻ as [HgI₄]²⁻ (Nessler-type complex). I₂ dissolves in iodide solution as the triiodide ion: I₂ + I⁻ → I₃⁻.', '{"a": "HgI₂ does not persist in excess iodide; it forms [HgI₄]²⁻.", "b": "Both parts are wrong: Hg ends as [HgI₄]²⁻ and I₂ as I₃⁻.", "d": "Hg(II) is not reduced to Hg₂²⁺."}'::jsonb, 'I₂ is poorly soluble in water but dissolves in KI as I₃⁻.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f4636b1a-6d21-5df4-87b3-afa8431bb960', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'The p-Block Elements', 'It is because of inability of ns² electrons of the valence shell to participate in bonding that :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Sn²⁺ is reducing while Pb⁴⁺ is oxidising"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sn²⁺ is oxidising while Pb⁴⁺ is reducing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sn²⁺ and Pb²⁺ are both oxidising and reducing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sn⁴⁺ is reducing while Pb⁴⁺ is oxidising"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The inert pair effect makes Pb²⁺ more stable than Pb⁴⁺, while for Sn the +4 state is more stable.', 'Down group 14 the ns² pair becomes reluctant to bond (inert pair effect), so the +2 state grows more stable. For Pb, +2 is the more stable state, so Pb⁴⁺ readily gains electrons and is oxidising. For Sn, +4 is still the more stable state, so Sn²⁺ readily loses electrons and is reducing.', '{"b": "Reverses both roles.", "c": "Pb²⁺ is stable; neither ion is both oxidising and reducing.", "d": "Sn⁴⁺ is the stable state and is not reducing."}'::jsonb, 'Inert pair effect: stability of lower oxidation state increases down groups 13–15.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('cb861733-c58a-59f0-9ff4-10a4046a599d', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 'Mechanism of a hypothetical reaction, X₂ + Y₂ → 2XY is given below :
(i) X₂ → X + X (fast)
(ii) X + Y₂ ⇌ XY + Y (slow)
(iii) X + Y → XY (fast)
The overall order of the reaction will be :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "0"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1.5"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Rate = k[X][Y₂] with [X] ∝ [X₂]^½, giving order 1.5.', 'The slow step (ii) controls the rate: rate = k[X][Y₂]. X comes from the fast pre-equilibrium X₂ ⇌ 2X, so [X] = (K[X₂])^½. Substituting gives rate = k′[X₂]^½[Y₂], so the overall order is ½ + 1 = 1.5.', '{"a": "Ignores the dependence on X₂.", "b": "Treats the overall stoichiometric equation as elementary.", "c": "The rate clearly depends on reactant concentrations."}'::jsonb, 'Intermediates in the rate-determining step are replaced using the fast pre-equilibrium.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('cf789d24-89b5-5c95-a284-9a96ee7d0ed8', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 'Concentration of the Ag⁺ ions in a saturated solution of Ag₂C₂O₄ is 2.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹. Solubility product of Ag₂C₂O₄ is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "2.42 × 10⁻⁸"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2.66 × 10⁻¹²"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4.5 × 10⁻¹¹"}, {"id": "d", "text": "5.3 × 10⁻¹²"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[C₂O₄²⁻] = (2.2×10⁻⁴)²(1.1×10⁻⁴) ≈ 5.3×10⁻¹².', 'Ag₂C₂O₄ ⇌ 2Ag⁺ + C₂O₄²⁻, so [C₂O₄²⁻] = ½[Ag⁺] = 1.1 × 10⁻⁴ M. Ksp = (2.2 × 10⁻⁴)² × 1.1 × 10⁻⁴ = 4.84 × 10⁻⁸ × 1.1 × 10⁻⁴ = 5.32 × 10⁻¹².', '{"a": "Product of the two ion concentrations without squaring [Ag⁺].", "b": "Uses s = 1.1×10⁻⁴ and forgets the factor of 4 in 4s³.", "c": "Arithmetic error."}'::jsonb, 'For A₂B type salts, Ksp = 4s³.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('bae6097b-29c1-5db3-81c8-25fbfed9c6d3', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Hydrocarbons', 'Which one is the correct order of acidity ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃–CH=CH₂ > CH₃–C≡CH > CH≡CH"}, {"id": "b", "text": "CH≡CH > CH₃–C≡CH > CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃–CH₃"}, {"id": "c", "text": "CH≡CH > CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃–C≡CH > CH₃–CH₃"}, {"id": "d", "text": "CH₃–CH₃ > CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃–C≡CH > CH≡CH"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'sp C–H is the most acidic; the methyl group in propyne lowers its acidity slightly below ethyne.', 'Acidity rises with s-character of the carbon bearing H: sp (50%) > sp² (33%) > sp³ (25%). So the alkynes are more acidic than ethene, and ethene is more acidic than ethane. Between the alkynes, the electron-donating CH₃ in propyne destabilises the acetylide, so ethyne > propyne.', '{"a": "Places alkenes above alkynes, the reverse of the s-character trend.", "c": "A terminal alkyne is more acidic than ethene.", "d": "The complete reverse order."}'::jsonb, 'Terminal alkynes are weakly acidic (react with Na, NaNH₂) because of sp C–H.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ee004d7a-5f03-5a7e-964a-1c9c69523ccb', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 'Consider the reactions : [reaction scheme shown in figure: X (C₂H₆O) –Cu/573 K→ A; A with [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺, OH⁻, Δ → silver mirror observed; A with OH⁻, Δ → Y; A with NH₂–NH–CO–NH₂ → Z]
Identify A, X, Y and Z.', '[{"id": "a", "text": "A-Methoxymethane, X-Ethanoic acid, Y-Acetate ion, Z-hydrazine."}, {"id": "b", "text": "A-Methoxymethane, X-Ethanol, Y-Ethanoic acid, Z-Semicarbazide."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A-Ethanal, X-Ethanol, Y-But-2-enal, Z-Semicarbazone."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A-Ethanol, X-Acetaldehyde, Y-Butanone, Z-Hydrazone."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Ethanol → (Cu, 573 K) ethanal, which gives the Tollens test, aldol-condenses to but-2-enal and forms a semicarbazone.', 'X (C₂H₆O) is dehydrogenated over hot Cu to an aldehyde, so X is ethanol and A is ethanal; the silver mirror confirms the aldehyde. Dilute alkali with heat gives aldol condensation: 2CH₃CHO → CH₃CH=CHCHO (but-2-enal). With semicarbazide, ethanal gives its semicarbazone, Z.', '{"a": "Methoxymethane cannot form by Cu dehydrogenation and gives no silver mirror.", "b": "Same problem with A, and semicarbazide is the reagent, not the product.", "d": "A must be the aldehyde, not ethanol, and the reagent gives a semicarbazone, not a hydrazone."}'::jsonb, 'Cu/573 K: 1° alcohol → aldehyde, 2° → ketone, 3° → alkene.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/174.webp'),
('f84fe918-aa15-5856-a7ca-fffb400a5cf3', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'The p-Block Elements', 'In which pair of ions both the species contain S–S bond ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "S₂O₇²⁻, S₂O₃²⁻"}, {"id": "b", "text": "S₄O₆²⁻, S₂O₃²⁻"}, {"id": "c", "text": "S₂O₇²⁻, S₂O₈²⁻"}, {"id": "d", "text": "S₄O₆²⁻, S₂O₇²⁻"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Thiosulphate has one S–S bond; tetrathionate has an S–S–S–S chain.', 'In S₂O₃²⁻ a terminal S is bonded directly to the central S. In S₄O₆²⁻ (⁻O₃S–S–S–SO₃⁻) there are S–S bonds. S₂O₇²⁻ has an S–O–S bridge and S₂O₈²⁻ has an O–O peroxo bridge, so neither has an S–S bond.', '{"a": "S₂O₇²⁻ (pyrosulphate) has an S–O–S link, not S–S.", "c": "Neither pyrosulphate nor peroxodisulphate has S–S.", "d": "S₂O₇²⁻ lacks an S–S bond."}'::jsonb, 'S₂O₈²⁻ has an O–O bond; S₂O₇²⁻ has S–O–S; S₂O₃²⁻ and S₄O₆²⁻ have S–S.', 'med', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('652111b5-7715-5245-869c-22735bda9427', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 'Which one is the most acidic compound ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Option (1) in figure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Option (2) in figure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Option (3) in figure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Option (4) in figure"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol) is the most acidic: three –NO₂ groups stabilise the phenoxide.', 'Electron-withdrawing –NO₂ groups at ortho and para positions delocalise the negative charge of the phenoxide ion by –R and –I effects, so acidity increases. The –CH₃ in p-cresol donates electrons and lowers acidity. The order is therefore 2,4,6-trinitrophenol > p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol.', '{"a": "p-Cresol is less acidic than phenol because CH₃ is electron-donating.", "b": "Phenol has no stabilising substituent.", "c": "One NO₂ increases acidity, but less than three."}'::jsonb, 'Picric acid (pKa ≈ 0.4) is nearly as strong as a mineral acid.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', '/pyq/ug/2017/176.webp'),
('ff8573e2-bf9b-5f5e-a862-a7ef43efc6ad', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 'In the electrochemical cell :
Zn|ZnSO₄ (0.01 M)||CuSO₄ (1.0 M)|Cu, the emf of this Daniel cell is E₁. When the concentration of ZnSO₄ is changed to 1.0 M and that of CuSO₄ changed to 0.01 M, the emf changes to E₂. From the followings, which one is the relationship between E₁ and E₂ ? (Given, RT/F = 0.059)', '[{"id": "a", "text": "E₁ = E₂"}, {"id": "b", "text": "E₁ < E₂"}, {"id": "c", "text": "E₁ > E₂"}, {"id": "d", "text": "E₂ = 0 ≠ E₁"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'E = E° − (0.059/2) log([Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺]); the first cell has the smaller quotient, so E₁ > E₂.', 'For Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, E = E° − (0.059/2) log([Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺]). Case 1: Q = 0.01/1 = 10⁻², so E₁ = E° + 0.059 V. Case 2: Q = 1/0.01 = 10², so E₂ = E° − 0.059 V. Hence E₁ > E₂.', '{"a": "The reaction quotient differs, so the emfs differ.", "b": "Reversed: raising [Zn²⁺] and lowering [Cu²⁺] decreases the emf.", "d": "E₂ = E° − 0.059 ≈ 1.04 V, not zero."}'::jsonb, 'Nernst: increasing product-ion concentration or decreasing reactant-ion concentration lowers cell emf.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('d42e2bf3-7109-569b-aecc-a52e1edcf838', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 'A first order reaction has a specific reaction rate of 10⁻² sec⁻¹. How much time will it take for 20 g of the reactant to reduce to 5 g ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "238.6 sec"}, {"id": "b", "text": "138.6 sec"}, {"id": "c", "text": "346.5 sec"}, {"id": "d", "text": "693.0 sec"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '20 g → 5 g is two half-lives: 2 × 0.693/10⁻² = 138.6 s.', 't½ = 0.693/k = 0.693/0.01 = 69.3 s. Going from 20 g to 10 g to 5 g takes two half-lives, so t = 138.6 s. Equivalently, t = (2.303/k) log(20/5) = 230.3 × 0.602 = 138.6 s.', '{"a": "Arithmetic slip.", "c": "This is five half-lives.", "d": "This is ten half-lives."}'::jsonb, 'First-order t½ = 0.693/k, independent of initial amount.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('f6f8de0f-d6e7-5d37-b2bf-3a462a79647a', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Organic Chemistry – Some Basic Principles and Techniques', 'The most suitable method of separation of 1 : 1 mixture of ortho and para-nitrophenols is :', '[{"id": "a", "text": "Sublimation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chromatography"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Crystallisation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Steam distillation"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'o-Nitrophenol (intramolecular H-bond) is steam volatile; p-nitrophenol (intermolecular H-bond) is not.', 'In o-nitrophenol the OH hydrogen-bonds to the adjacent NO₂ within the same molecule, so the molecules are less associated and volatile in steam. p-Nitrophenol forms intermolecular hydrogen bonds and stays behind. Steam distillation therefore separates them cleanly.', '{"a": "Neither isomer sublimes conveniently for separation.", "b": "Chromatography works, but steam distillation is the standard bulk method.", "c": "Their solubilities are not different enough for practical crystallisation."}'::jsonb, 'Steam distillation separates steam-volatile, water-immiscible compounds (e.g., o-nitrophenol, aniline).', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL),
('ec85a3a6-32d3-505e-8c87-6eb8a25c1313', 'neet_ug', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure', 'Which one of the following pairs of species have the same bond order ?', '[{"id": "a", "text": "CO, NO"}, {"id": "b", "text": "O₂, NO⁺"}, {"id": "c", "text": "CN⁻, CO"}, {"id": "d", "text": "N₂, O₂⁻"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'CN⁻ and CO are both isoelectronic with N₂ (14 electrons), so both have bond order 3.', 'By MO theory, a 14-electron diatomic has bond order (10 − 4)/2 = 3. CN⁻ (6 + 7 + 1 = 14) and CO (6 + 8 = 14) both have 14 electrons, so each has bond order 3. In the other pairs, CO = 3 vs NO = 2.5; O₂ = 2 vs NO⁺ = 3; N₂ = 3 vs O₂⁻ = 1.5.', '{"a": "NO (15 e⁻) has bond order 2.5.", "b": "O₂ has 2, NO⁺ has 3.", "d": "O₂⁻ has 1.5, N₂ has 3."}'::jsonb, 'Isoelectronic diatomics (N₂, CO, CN⁻, NO⁺) have the same bond order.', 'easy', true, 2017, NULL, 'NEET UG 2017 (NTA) · answer verified by UG Prep', NULL)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
